item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;Google "best resume format" and you'll get about 40 different answers. Some say functional. Some say creative. Some sites want you to buy a $29 template with a sidebar, a photo circle and little skill bars for every language you've ever touched.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhu9tdpaxn3yvhdg45z98.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhu9tdpaxn3yvhdg45z98.png" alt=" " width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Here's the honest answer. For most developers in 2026 the best resume format is reverse chronological. It's not exciting but it's what recruiters expect and what hiring software parses best.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That said, it's not the right pick for everyone. Bootcamp grads, career switchers and new grads have some different options. So let's go through the three main formats, who each one is for and what they actually look like.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


The 3 resume formats, quickly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are really only three you need to know about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reverse chronological&lt;/strong&gt; lists your jobs starting with the most recent one. &lt;strong&gt;Functional&lt;/strong&gt; groups your experience by skill and mostly hides the timeline. &lt;strong&gt;Combination&lt;/strong&gt; (some people call it hybrid) puts a short skills section on top, then your jobs in reverse chronological order underneath.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's it. Everything else is just a theme on top of one of these.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


1. Reverse chronological: the default for a reason
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the format most recruiters read all day. Their eyes go straight to three things: your most recent job title, the company and the dates. This format puts all three right where they're looking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's also the easiest format for an ATS to parse, because each job is a clean block with a title, company, dates and bullets. Think of it like well structured data versus a blob of text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice it goes like this. Your name, contact details and GitHub link at the top, then a short summary, then your experience with the newest job first. Each job gets a title, company, city, dates and three to five bullets that show real results, like cutting API latency or shipping a migration. Skills and education sit underneath.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; anyone with steady dev experience. If you've been writing code professionally for two or more years, use this and stop overthinking it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


2. Functional: why most recruiters aren't fans
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A functional resume groups everything under skills like "Frontend" or "DevOps" and either buries your job history at the bottom or leaves the dates out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On paper it sounds smart if you have gaps or you're switching into tech. In practice a lot of recruiters see it and wonder what you're hiding. And ATS software has a harder time connecting your skills to actual jobs because the two are separated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honestly in 2026 there's very few situations where functional is the best choice. If you're tempted, the combination format below almost always does the job better.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


3. Combination: best for career switchers and bootcamp grads
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The combination format gives you a few lines at the top to highlight your strongest technical skills, then shows your experience in normal reverse chronological order. So the recruiter sees what you can build first and your timeline second, without anything looking hidden.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say you spent five years as an accountant and just finished a bootcamp. You'd open with a few lines on your stack and what you've built, then a projects section, then your accounting jobs underneath. That finance background is actually a selling point for fintech teams, so mention it up top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The skills and projects connect your new tech skills to your old career before the reader even gets to "Accountant."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; career switchers, bootcamp grads, people coming back after a gap and newcomers to Canada whose overseas job titles might not be familiar to a local recruiter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


A quick way to pick yours
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've got two or more years of dev experience go with reverse chronological. If you're switching into tech or have a big gap go with combination. If you're a student use reverse chronological but put education and projects first since that's your strongest stuff right now. And functional? Pretty much never.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Formatting rules that matter more than the format
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the thing most people miss. Picking the "right" format won't save a resume that's hard to parse. These details matter just as much.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stick to &lt;strong&gt;one column&lt;/strong&gt;. Use &lt;strong&gt;standard headings&lt;/strong&gt; like Experience, Projects, Education and Skills so software knows what each section is. Keep the font simple, Calibri, Arial or Garamond at 10 to 12 points.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write your GitHub and portfolio links as plain text URLs. An icon of the GitHub logo means nothing to a parser. And put them in the body of the page, not the header, since some systems skip headers completely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For length, one page is right for most devs with under five years of experience. Two pages max for everyone else. Save as PDF unless the posting asks for Word.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Format mistakes that quietly cost you interviews
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The big one is &lt;strong&gt;two column templates&lt;/strong&gt;. They look great, but a lot of ATS software reads them left to right across both columns and scrambles your info. Your skills end up in the middle of a job description.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skip the &lt;strong&gt;photo&lt;/strong&gt; if you're applying in Canada or the US. Skip &lt;strong&gt;skill bars&lt;/strong&gt; too. "Python ●●●○○" doesn't tell anyone anything. Is three dots "can write a script" or "can't write a decorator"? Nobody knows and software can't read it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And don't list 30 technologies. If you can't talk about it for five minutes in an interview, take it off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick test. Open your PDF, select all, copy it and paste it into a plain text file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Good: sections in order, dates next to the right job, links readable
Bad:  columns merged, skills mixed into experience, contact info missing
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If it looks bad thats what the ATS sees too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Use a template that gets the format right for you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to build any of this from scratch. If you want a head start, &lt;a href="https://resumestart.ai/resume-examples" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ResumeStart&lt;/a&gt; has 30+ clean resume designs that are built to be easy for both people and ATS software to read. You can switch designs any time without retyping anything, and the PDF you download looks exactly like what you see on screen. It's free to start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The bottom line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best resume format isn't the fanciest one. It's the one a recruiter can scan in a few seconds and an ATS can parse without breaking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For most devs thats reverse chronological. If you're switching careers or coming from a bootcamp, use combination. Keep it to one column, keep it simple and spend the time you saved on better project bullets. That's what gets you the interview.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a free HTML5 games portal, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://playcombo.it" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SPC – Super Play Combo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. It's built on WordPress with the GeneratePress theme and has over 3,000 games from the GameMonetize and GamePix feeds. Over the last few weeks I rebuilt the homepage in a Poki-like style, with one horizontal row per category. It looked easy. It wasn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are the real problems I ran into and how I fixed them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Category rows with no duplicates
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each category (Puzzle, Arcade, Match-3, Mahjong, Quiz, Sports, Racing…) has its own page with its own game list. The homepage shows one row per category, built by a script called &lt;code&gt;motore-playcombo.js&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feeds use English category names, while my site uses Italian ones, so I made a map:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CATEGORIE_HOME&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;puzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;sparabolle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;arcade&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;match-3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;mahjong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;trivia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;gamesforgirls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;sports&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;racing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;adventure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;platformer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2048&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Golden rule: &lt;strong&gt;each game appears in exactly one row&lt;/strong&gt;. A &lt;code&gt;Set&lt;/code&gt; of already-used ids does the job:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;alreadyShown&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeDuplicates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;games&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;games&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alreadyShown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;has&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alreadyShown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Rows further down only load when they're about to scroll into view:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;category&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unobserve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;300px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


2. "It works when I'm logged in, but not in incognito"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Quiz and Card rows were full when I was logged in as admin, but &lt;strong&gt;empty for visitors&lt;/strong&gt;. The culprit was LiteSpeed Cache. For visitors who aren't logged in, it optimizes the page and turns the game list inside the HTML into &lt;strong&gt;base64&lt;/strong&gt;. My script was looking for plain text and found nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is to try decoding it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;atob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lesson:&lt;/strong&gt; always test in incognito. What you see as an admin is not what your users see.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. The little &lt;code&gt;?v=&lt;/code&gt; number that saves your life
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The script is loaded like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;src=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/js/motore-playcombo.js?v=16"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;?v=16&lt;/code&gt; does nothing to the code. It only tells the browser (and the cache, and the PWA service worker) that the file is new. Every time I upload a new version, I:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;bump the number on &lt;strong&gt;every&lt;/strong&gt; page that loads the script;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;purge the LiteSpeed cache.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forget this and you'll get 404 errors for files you already deleted, because the browser is still running the old version. Ask me how I know.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Thumbnails: from wsrv.nl to i0.wp.com
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make thumbnails lighter, I was running them through an image proxy. Then I found out that the service's &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; blocks Googlebot, so Google couldn't see my images. I switched to WordPress's free image CDN:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;optimizeImg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;gamepix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`https://i0.wp.com/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;host&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;?w=240&amp;amp;quality=75`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also added a small safety net: if the optimized image fails to load, it falls back to the original.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onerror&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onerror&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;original&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The result is a &lt;strong&gt;much&lt;/strong&gt; faster homepage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. A bit of (lightweight) style
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One last touch: the background has game-themed icons (a joypad, a coin, a star, a heart) made with pure CSS and no shadows, so phones don't struggle. With &lt;code&gt;body.page-id-XXXX&lt;/code&gt; I can give each page its own look, like floating yellow stars only on the Girls page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Always&lt;/strong&gt; test in incognito and on a phone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The cache is your friend, until you forget about it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A wrong (or unchanged) &lt;code&gt;?v=&lt;/code&gt; can cost you hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check the &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; of any third-party service you rely on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;PWA errors often go away by themselves after a few minutes, so don't panic right away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to see the result, the games are on &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://playcombo.it" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;playcombo.it&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Free, as always. 🎮&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask Claude for a landing page and you tend to get the same thing: a warm cream background, a terracotta accent, identical rounded cards with soft shadows and an all-caps eyebrow above every heading. The &lt;a href="https://github.com/anthropics/skills" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;frontend-design skill&lt;/a&gt; in Anthropic's example skills repo exists to break that habit. Below is the route with the real commands. The short version is on the &lt;a href="https://dedupedai.com/use-cases/anthropics-skills/build-distinctive-frontends-with-the-frontend-design-skill/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=howto_frontenddesign" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Deduped use-case page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the skill does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skills are folders of instructions, scripts and resources that Claude loads when a task calls for them. frontend-design makes Claude commit to a bold aesthetic direction and a reviewed design plan before it writes any UI code. It even lists the generic defaults to avoid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Install
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Claude Code, register the repo as a plugin marketplace:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;/plugin marketplace add anthropics/skills
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then install the example skills, which include frontend-design:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;/plugin install example-skills@anthropic-agent-skills
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Give it a real brief
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tell Claude the real subject, the audience and the primary job of the page. If the brief leaves the subject out, the skill has Claude propose one and confirm it with you, so you will not get lorem ipsum branding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Ask for the plan first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before any code, ask for a design plan:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4 to 6 named hex colors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the typefaces and their roles&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a layout concept with ASCII wireframes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the principles that make this page unique&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Review the plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have Claude check the plan against your brief and rewrite any part that reads like a generic default, saying what changed and why. This one step is where most of the improvement comes from, because it is cheap to change a plan and expensive to change a finished page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check the quality floor
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result should be responsive down to mobile, show visible keyboard focus and respect reduced motion. Test each of those yourself before shipping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A prompt you can paste
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Use the frontend-design skill from anthropics/skills to design a landing page for my product. Before writing any code, give me a design plan: 4 to 6 named hex colors, the typefaces and their roles, a layout concept with an ASCII wireframe, and the principles. Review the plan against my brief, revise anything that reads like a generic default and tell me what you changed. Spend the boldness in one place, and keep it responsive down to mobile with visible keyboard focus and reduced motion respected.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Who it is for
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer or founder who keeps getting the same templated look from generated UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Tip
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Spend the boldness in one place. A page with one memorable element and a calm everything else usually beats a page where every section shouts.&lt;/p&gt;

