&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run it yourself&lt;/strong&gt; (needs &lt;a href="https://ollama.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ollama&lt;/a&gt; and Node.js 18+):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ollama pull moondream
ollama pull qwen2.5:3b
git clone https://github.com/shristi005/Miedocuentista.git
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;Miedocuentista/backend
npm start
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then open &lt;code&gt;http://localhost:3002&lt;/code&gt;. There is no &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt; step; the backend has zero dependencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Open models (via Ollama, all local):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;moondream&lt;/strong&gt; (about 1.7 GB, open weights): the "eyes". For each frame it answers four short questions (caption, objects, setting, mood). Small vision models follow short questions far better than one long prompt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;qwen2.5:3b&lt;/strong&gt; (about 1.9 GB, open weights): the "writer", the "memory keeper" and the "editor".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;App:&lt;/strong&gt; Node.js backend with &lt;strong&gt;zero npm dependencies&lt;/strong&gt;, plain HTML/CSS/JS frontend, and one JSON file per project. Frontend and backend are separate folders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The parts I'm proudest of:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rolling story memory.&lt;/strong&gt; A 3B model on a small GPU can't re-read a novel for every scene. After each scene, the model merges the old memory with the new scene into a note of about 140 words (characters, setting, events, open threads). The note stays the same size however long the story gets, and only the previous scene's ending is passed word for word.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Regenerate / undo that restores the story memory.&lt;/strong&gt; Each scene saves the memory from before it, so regenerating or undoing the last scene brings back the exact earlier state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Final AI Story Editor.&lt;/strong&gt; Pass 1 reads the complete story and writes an edit report (canon, plot holes, timeline errors, repetition, weak transitions, per-scene fixes). Pass 2 rewrites each scene using that report plus the neighbouring scenes. The original is always kept, with an Edited/Original toggle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human in the loop.&lt;/strong&gt; The vision model makes mistakes, so every frame's caption and element chips are editable before they shape the story.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Challenges I Hit (and Why I Think They Happen)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I tested with a three-frame lighthouse story: a boat and pier, a stone archway ringed with red roses and an urn, and a keeper's room with a journal. The pipeline worked end to end (image to elements to scene to memory to next scene to editor), but a 3B model on a 4 GB GPU has real limits. I'd rather show them than hide them. The causes below are my best diagnosis from reading the outputs and my own prompts, not proven facts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Problem I saw&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it looked like&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Likely cause&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Planned fix&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Copy-pasting earlier text&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The same paragraphs ("The room spun, making her dizzy for a moment…", "The handprint on the urn matched the others…") show up in all three chapters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;My prompt passes the last ~1,200 characters of the previous scene word for word. Small models tend to copy what is sitting in their context, and I set no repetition penalty&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pass a one-line summary of the last beat instead of raw text; set a repeat penalty; detect duplicated sentences and regenerate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The story stalls and loops&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chapters keep re-describing the lantern, the midnight, the room and the handprint instead of moving on&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;I tell the writer to use every visible element, neighbouring frames overlap, and the model has no plan for what NEW thing happens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A "beat planning" step: decide one new event and one new piece of information, then write from that plan&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory feeds on itself&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Repetition gets worse in later chapters, and the timeline resets (she arrives at the dock again)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The story memory is summarised by the same small model from text that is already repetitive, so the repetition is baked into the memory. There is no location/time tracking&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Structured memory (character sheet, event list, current place and time), facts the user can pin, and de-duplication before summarising&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Headings and numbering ignored&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two blocks labelled "Chapter 2" and a third with no heading; chapter titles reuse the book's title&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;I ask the model to write the heading itself, and 3B models follow formatting loosely&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The app inserts the chapter number; a separate tiny call writes a new chapter title&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prompt wording leaks into the prose&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Phrases like "the journal from the previous scene" appear inside the story&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Labels in my prompt (PREVIOUS SCENE, reader-added elements) get echoed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rename labels, clean leaked phrases after generation, add a worked example&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Script mode leaks its template&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;NAME:&lt;/code&gt; printed literally, &lt;code&gt;CUT TO / FADE OUT / SMASH CUT&lt;/code&gt; copied as-is, the prompt's input block echoed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;I used placeholders in the format instructions and the model copied them&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Replace placeholders with one concrete example scene; strip stray labels&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Final Editor can't fix it&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The edited version still has repeated passages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The editor is the same small model that wrote the problems, so it cannot reliably spot them&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A deterministic de-duplication pass first; a larger model for the editor only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vision mistakes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The 1.7 GB vision model sometimes misses or invents objects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tiny model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Already mitigated: every caption and chip is editable before it shapes the story. Next: a stronger vision model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What this taught me.&lt;/strong&gt; Small local models are good at structure and weak at long, coherent prose. Most of what went wrong is engineering around the model (what I put in its context, what I let it decide, what I check afterwards), not a flaw in the idea. The rolling memory and two-pass editor are my first answer to that; the table above is the second round.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Future Improvements
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quality guardrails:&lt;/strong&gt; a repetition score for every scene, automatic regeneration when a scene copies earlier text, and a "must change something" rule for each scene.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Planning before writing:&lt;/strong&gt; generate a short beat (new event, new information, emotional turn) from the story memory and the frame, then write the scene from that beat.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Better memory:&lt;/strong&gt; structured JSON memory with a character sheet, timeline and open threads, plus facts the author can pin so names and relationships never drift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stricter formats:&lt;/strong&gt; the app owns chapter numbers and scene headings; script output is cleaned and validated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bigger or better models:&lt;/strong&gt; because the models are environment variables, a 7B or 8B model (on a machine with more VRAM) can be dropped in. I want to benchmark a few open models side by side on the same frames.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In-browser editing&lt;/strong&gt; of any scene, and a side-by-side diff between original and edited versions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Video-workflow hand-off:&lt;/strong&gt; export each scene as a shot list (&lt;code&gt;VISUAL&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SOUND&lt;/code&gt;, dialogue, duration) so open-source image/video and text-to-speech tools can pick it up. &lt;strong&gt;Not built yet.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A Día de los Muertos "ofrenda" mode:&lt;/strong&gt; remembrance stories from old family photos, kept fully on the owner's machine. Today the Día de los Muertos option is only a tone setting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How It Can Be Used
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Miedocuentista is best treated as a &lt;strong&gt;first-draft and idea engine&lt;/strong&gt;, not a finished-story generator. You get structure and momentum quickly, then you edit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Short films and YouTube videos:&lt;/strong&gt; drop in storyboard frames or concept art, get a scene-by-scene script with visuals, sound and dialogue to rewrite. Later, the same structure is meant to feed AI video workflows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Novel and short-story outlines:&lt;/strong&gt; turn a mood board or a folder of reference images into chapters to react to.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Game and world-building:&lt;/strong&gt; turn level screenshots or location art into lore and quest text.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Classrooms and writing groups:&lt;/strong&gt; the same photos, different frames, different stories; compare and discuss.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Private projects:&lt;/strong&gt; because everything is local, it is suitable for personal photos and unreleased art.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How:&lt;/strong&gt; (1) pick short film, feature film or novel; (2) choose script or story; (3) add your frames one by one and correct the elements; (4) mark the last frame; (5) run the Final Editor; (6) export &lt;code&gt;.txt&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;.md&lt;/code&gt; and finish by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your photos stay yours.&lt;/strong&gt; Stories get built from personal images: old family photos, sketches, unreleased concept art. With local open-weight models, none of it is uploaded anywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It works with the lights (and the internet) off.&lt;/strong&gt; No API key, no rate limit, no per-token bill. The UI even avoids external fonts and CDNs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's not locked inside a service that might disappear.&lt;/strong&gt; Projects are plain JSON files I own. If a hosted API changes its pricing or shuts down, my stories and my workflow survive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Swappable models.&lt;/strong&gt; The vision and story models are environment variables, so another open model can be tried without touching the code, and a stronger machine should give better stories. (So far I have only tested moondream and qwen2.5:3b.)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It made a tiny-model design possible.&lt;/strong&gt; The rolling memory and two-pass editor exist &lt;em&gt;because&lt;/em&gt; I had to make a 3B model on a 4 GB GPU work. A closed API with a huge context window would have hidden that problem instead of letting me solve it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Free to run,&lt;/strong&gt; which matters for students and indie creators.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Spirit of It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I gave it three images: a boat with a lantern at a lighthouse pier, a stone archway ringed with red roses, and a keeper's room with a journal. Out of those three photos, with no plot from me, it wrote a novel in my first test run whose final chapter ends on a twist I did not plan: the light the heroine had been chasing for fifteen years was her missing brother all along.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The outputs from my test runs (problems included) are in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/shristi005/Miedocuentista/tree/main/tested_outputs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;tested_outputs&lt;/code&gt; folder&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/shristi005/Miedocuentista/blob/main/tested_outputs/the-keeper-s-last-light-novel.txt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Novel output&lt;/a&gt; (3 chapters)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/shristi005/Miedocuentista/blob/main/tested_outputs/the-keeper-s-last-light-short-film-script.txt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Short film script output&lt;/a&gt; (3 scenes; the weakest result, see the Challenges section)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/shristi005/Miedocuentista/tree/main/tested_outputs/input_frame_images" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The three input frame images&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Wiz Runtime Sensor is a monitoring component from Wiz that watches what running containers actually do inside a Kubernetes cluster, at kernel level.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Google Cloud announced it on 17 October 2026 as a managed add-on for Google Kubernetes Engine, in preview, for both Standard and Autopilot clusters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GKE installs the sensor, injects its credentials from Secret Manager and updates it outside the cluster version cycle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A custom organization policy can reject any new cluster created without the add-on, which moves enforcement to the platform team.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The sensor reads system calls, processes, network connections and file access, with a fixed memory limit on each node, and sends that telemetry to the Wiz Security Graph.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preview status, the egress proxy path and the gap between a GKE-managed and a Helm-managed install are the first things an operator should verify.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Runtime security has been the awkward purchase in Kubernetes for years. Every cluster needs an agent, every agent needs a credential, and the team that owns the clusters is rarely the team that owns the agent's licence. Google Cloud and Wiz have now put the agent inside the platform itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The announcement is short and specific: the Wiz Runtime Sensor becomes a first-party add-on for GKE, in preview, covering Standard and Autopilot clusters, with GKE handling deployment, credential injection and updates. The detection engine is the visible part of the release. The release also moves credential handling and fleet enforcement into the platform, changing the operating model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Runtime Sensor Watches What Containers Do at Kernel Level
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A container image can be scanned before it runs, and a manifest can be checked before it is admitted. Runtime observation covers processes that begin after an application launches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Wiz Runtime Sensor covers that window. It observes system calls, running processes, network connections and file access. The GKE documentation demonstrates a reverse-shell alert, while the Wiz engineering post lists reverse shells, binary execution from /tmp, image drift and privilege-escalation attempts. Processing happens on the node through an eBPF program, so the library does not need to be linked into the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resource use is bounded on purpose. The sensor keeps a fixed memory limit on each node, which matters on a cluster where workload density changes through the day, and the announcement points at AI workloads and web services as the cases where that predictability helps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy995n5l59c6cjy6bqmyf.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy995n5l59c6cjy6bqmyf.jpg" alt="Wiz Sensor for GKE architecture diagram, control plane to graph — cloud.google.com/blog/products/identity-security" width="800" height="447"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture figure published with the Google Cloud announcement (retrieved 28 October 2026, cloud.google.com/blog/products/identity-security/wiz-sensor-for-gke-turnkey-threat-detection-for-kubernetes), showing credential sync, sensor deployment and the threat telemetry path back to Wiz.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


GKE Installs the Sensor and Pipes Credentials From Secret Manager
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part of this release that changes an operating model is the credential path. Distributing a vendor API key into a namespace as a Kubernetes Secret is the standard shortcut, and it is the step that turns a security product into a secret to rotate at scale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here the key lives outside the cluster. GKE uses its Secret Manager secret sync integration to hold the Wiz credentials in a central vault that only a dedicated service account can read, then retrieves them and injects them into the sensor pods. Updating a key in Secret Manager automatically re-synchronizes the Kubernetes Secret. Using updated registry or API credentials then requires a manual restart of the Runtime Sensor DaemonSet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GKE documentation adds the mechanical detail: enabling the add-on creates the &lt;code&gt;gke-managed-wiz&lt;/code&gt; namespace, installs the Runtime Sensor components there, and pulls the images from the Wiz private container registry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjb09r4zckejz5ufvu4sf.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjb09r4zckejz5ufvu4sf.webp" alt="Wiz diagram of the four layers behind the GKE sensor — wiz.io/blog/wiz-sensor-on-gke-1-click-runtime-protection-now-native" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wiz's own view of the chain (retrieved 28 October 2026, wiz.io/blog/wiz-sensor-on-gke-1-click-runtime-protection-now-native): the GKE control plane, Google Cloud Secret Manager, the node-level eBPF engine and the Wiz Security Graph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One gcloud Flag Enables It on a New or Existing Cluster
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enabling the sensor follows the path a platform administrator already knows. On a new cluster the announcement documents a single flag on the cluster create command, &lt;code&gt;--addons=WizSensor=ENABLED&lt;/code&gt;, alongside the two Secret Manager references that name the registry secret and the API key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a running cluster the equivalent is &lt;code&gt;--update-addons=WizSensor=ENABLED&lt;/code&gt;, and the prerequisites are explicit: Workload Identity Federation for GKE and Secret Manager secret sync have to be in place before any of it works. That pairing is the real gate, since a cluster without workload identity cannot present the service account the vault checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams that restrict outbound traffic, the announcement documents standard HTTP and mutual TLS proxy configurations so telemetry reaches Wiz while egress firewall rules stay intact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fktrhrbxu36pnzx7w0dmm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fktrhrbxu36pnzx7w0dmm.png" alt="Install page for the Wiz sensor add-on — docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/security/install-wiz-sensor-addon" width="800" height="600"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Page capture of the GKE installation documentation (retrieved 28 October 2026, docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/security/install-wiz-sensor-addon), which lists the prerequisites and the namespace the add-on creates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


An Organization Policy Makes Runtime Coverage a Create-Time Condition
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fleet-wide enforcement is where most runtime programmes fail. A team enables the agent on the clusters it knows about, and the cluster somebody spins up on a Friday afternoon arrives without it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The add-on is designed to close that gap through Organization Policy Service custom constraints. The announcement publishes the constraint itself: a resource type of &lt;code&gt;container.googleapis.com/Cluster&lt;/code&gt;, the method types CREATE and UPDATE, and a condition that the cluster's addon configuration has the sensor enabled. Apply it at the organization, folder or project level and a cluster created without runtime protection is refused.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read that constraint as a governance instrument. It makes agent installation an API-enforced rule that platform teams can apply when clusters are created or updated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fehh3sjrafnmf1bnf6jg0.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fehh3sjrafnmf1bnf6jg0.webp" alt="Wiz console showing an active GKE sensor deployment — wiz.io/blog/wiz-sensor-on-gke-1-click-runtime-protection-now-native" width="800" height="446"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Wiz console view from the vendor's own post (retrieved 28 October 2026, wiz.io/blog/wiz-sensor-on-gke-1-click-runtime-protection-now-native) showing a sensor deployment reported by GKE with its active sensor pods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two practical notes about the constraint. It evaluates the cluster's add-on configuration at create and update time, so a later change to that configuration is checked as well as a brand new cluster. Applying it at the folder level covers the environments nobody remembers to check, which is where most coverage gaps start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Telemetry Reaches the Wiz Security Graph and Answers a Narrower Question
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Detection rules answer specific questions about runtime activity. Image scanning identifies vulnerable libraries in an image and supplies posture context. Runtime evidence shows which of those libraries are loaded and executing in memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The announcement frames the Wiz Security Graph as the place where those two views meet: cluster runtime activity correlated with image scans, cloud posture and identity. The practical effect is prioritisation around vulnerable code that is loaded and running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Diagnostic data is collected when a threat fires, so an investigation starts with evidence attached.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpahqk8bttyzmwgtlzr7z.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpahqk8bttyzmwgtlzr7z.png" alt="Google Cloud announcement page for the Wiz sensor add-on — cloud.google.com/blog/products/identity-security" width="800" height="600"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Page capture of the Google Cloud announcement (retrieved 28 October 2026, cloud.google.com/blog/products/identity-security/wiz-sensor-for-gke-turnkey-threat-detection-for-kubernetes), the source of the enablement flags and the organization policy above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Independent Updates Keep Detection Rules Ahead of the Cluster Version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent lifecycle is the operational tax of runtime security. Every agent image is a new thing to test, and every change usually waits for a maintenance window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GKE documentation states the arrangement directly: "Any updates to the Runtime Sensor components are independent of GKE patch version upgrades." The announcement adds that sensor versions and detection rules published by Wiz roll out without GKE version bumps, node pool recreation or workload disruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That independence cuts both ways, and a platform team should decide which way it wants before enabling anything. A detection engine that updates on the vendor's schedule keeps pace with new attack behaviour. It also changes what runs on your nodes outside your release calendar, which is a governance question for a regulated environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxdgeenaehy99lz2mn9ms.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxdgeenaehy99lz2mn9ms.webp" alt="Netics visual: what GKE manages and what the operator keeps" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Netics editorial visual: the split between what the managed add-on now owns and what stays with the platform team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Preview Label, the Proxy and the Helm Alternative Are the First Checks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three details decide whether this is a production option this quarter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first is the preview status. GKE documentation places the feature under the Pre-GA terms of service, which means the usual "available as is" language and limited support commitments. A regulated workload can wait, and the wait is cheap because nothing forces an early decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second is egress design. A sensor that cannot reach Wiz reports nothing, so the proxy configuration is part of the architecture and belongs in the firewall request early. The third is the install path. The Helm chart remains available, offers more configuration freedom, and leaves the components unmanaged by GKE, with issues reported to Wiz. Choose per estate: the add-on for fleet coverage, the chart for clusters that need tuning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider a hypothetical case. Imagine a Paris platform team running forty clusters across three environments and two cloud projects. The add-on plus one organization constraint covers the fleet in a week, and the two clusters running bespoke networking stay on the chart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93gac4crmgus1f5bjlj8.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93gac4crmgus1f5bjlj8.webp" alt="Netics visual: the three checks before enabling the sensor add-on" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Netics editorial visual: the preview status, the egress path and the install path, the three checks that come before any rollout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where Runtime Coverage Fits Beside the Configuration Review
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Runtime detection complements the controls that decide what a workload may do before it starts. Admission policies, least-privilege service accounts, network rules and the sensor work together to record configured boundaries and activity inside them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The add-on makes that layer cheaper to run, and the enforcement constraint makes it harder to forget. Both are good news for a small platform team that has been maintaining three copies of an agent across a growing fleet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The open question is what the platform team gives up in exchange for the managed lifecycle: a vendor-owned update channel on every node, and a detection engine that lives outside the cluster's own release process. That trade is reasonable for most estates, and it deserves an explicit answer taken on purpose. This level of hardening belongs in &lt;a href="https://neticslabs.com/en/services/security" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;security audit and hardening&lt;/a&gt; work, and the credential-handling argument connects to the earlier analysis of &lt;a href="https://blog.neticslabs.com/fortinet-hardening-release-ai-assisted/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hardening a Fortinet estate with AI assistance&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://blog.neticslabs.com/gke-wiz-runtime-sensor-managed-add-on/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the Netics blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 30 at 17:50 UTC, GitHub started &lt;a href="https://www.githubstatus.com/incidents/pwxsjgnvrk6b" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;investigating degraded performance for Actions and Webhooks&lt;/a&gt;. Ten minutes later Pages was on the list too. By 18:06 they were reporting delayed workflows, delayed Pages deployments, delayed webhook events, and errors in code search and live updates. The incident was marked resolved at 19:23.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's about 90 minutes on the status page. In the &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50307723" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hacker News thread&lt;/a&gt;, one commenter pointed out that the same incident happened 11 days earlier, and another counted "several Actions outages in the past two weeks."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same day, Tangled published &lt;a href="https://tangled.org/blog/forge/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The federated code forge&lt;/a&gt;, arguing that relying on someone else for your uptime "hasn't played out well." I don't think the timing was planned. It still works as an ad.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My take: don't move everything off GitHub because of one bad Friday afternoon. Spend an hour this week mirroring your repos to Codeberg, a self-hosted Forgejo or Tangled, so the next outage costs you 10 minutes instead of a lost evening. Below: a status check you can alias, four ways to mirror, the popular setup that fails during an outage, and a CI fallback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 0: check if GitHub is down before you debug your own pipeline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've seen people (fine, I've been one of them) rerun a stuck workflow four times, roll back a commit and blame a Dependabot bump before checking whether GitHub itself is broken. GitHub's status page has a public JSON API, so make the check a shell alias:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;alias &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;ghdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'curl -s https://www.githubstatus.com/api/v2/components.json \
| jq -r ".components[] | select(.status != \"operational\") | \"\(.name): \(.status)\""'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If that prints &lt;code&gt;Actions: degraded_performance&lt;/code&gt;, close the laptop. To list active incidents, use &lt;code&gt;https://www.githubstatus.com/api/v2/incidents/unresolved.json&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;a href="https://www.githubstatus.com/api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full endpoint list&lt;/a&gt; also covers summary, scheduled maintenance and incident history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One caveat from the HN thread: people noticed the 30-day uptime bar staying green during acknowledged incidents, because partial degradation isn't counted as downtime. One commenter mentioned that a third-party status page had to be built to track Actions accurately. So a green bar on the status page tells you very little.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The trap: mirroring GitHub with a GitHub Action
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most common mirror setup I see is a workflow that runs &lt;code&gt;git push --mirror&lt;/code&gt; to Codeberg on every push. It works fine until Actions goes down, and then the mirror stops updating at exactly the moment you need it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your mirror has to be updated by something that doesn't depend on GitHub's job queue. That leaves two options: your own machine, or a forge that pushes or pulls on its own schedule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Option 1: one &lt;code&gt;git push&lt;/code&gt;, two forges
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the cheapest setup and needs no infrastructure. Git lets one remote have several push URLs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# keep fetching from GitHub, but push everywhere&lt;/span&gt;
git remote set-url &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--add&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--push&lt;/span&gt; origin git@github.com:you/project.git
git remote set-url &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--add&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--push&lt;/span&gt; origin git@codeberg.org:you/project.git
git remote set-url &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--add&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--push&lt;/span&gt; origin git@tangled.org:you.tngl.sh/project

git remote &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-v&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# origin  git@github.com:you/project.git (fetch)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# origin  git@github.com:you/project.git (push)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# origin  git@codeberg.org:you/project.git (push)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# origin  git@tangled.org:you.tngl.sh/project (push)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first &lt;code&gt;--add --push&lt;/code&gt; replaces the implicit push URL, which is why GitHub has to be listed explicitly. From then on, every &lt;code&gt;git push&lt;/code&gt; goes to all three. You need to create the empty repos on Codeberg and Tangled first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weakness is that it only mirrors what you push from that clone. Merges done through the GitHub UI won't show up until someone pulls and pushes again. For a solo project that's fine. For a team, use option 3.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Option 2: Codeberg, which only takes pushed mirrors
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Codeberg runs Forgejo, and Forgejo supports pull mirrors. Codeberg.org doesn't allow them, though. Their FAQ says that "mirrors that pull content from other code hosting services were problematic for Codeberg" and suggests manual mirrors with multiple remotes and &lt;code&gt;git push --mirror&lt;/code&gt; instead (&lt;a href="https://docs.codeberg.org/getting-started/faq/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt;). That's option 1 again, and I think it's a reasonable rule for a nonprofit that pays for its own disks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the Codeberg copy is a backup and nothing more, turn off issues and PRs in the repo settings so nobody files bugs in the wrong place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Option 3: self-hosted Forgejo pull mirror, then fan out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a $5 VPS or a homelab box, this is the setup I'd pick, because it's the only one here that catches merges done in the GitHub UI. On your own Forgejo instance, create the repo with &lt;strong&gt;New Migration&lt;/strong&gt; and check &lt;strong&gt;This repository will be a mirror&lt;/strong&gt;. Forgejo then pulls from GitHub on a schedule, including changes made through the GitHub web UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can then fan out from there: &lt;strong&gt;Settings &amp;gt; Repository &amp;gt; Mirror Settings &amp;gt; Add Push Mirror&lt;/strong&gt; pushes to Codeberg, Tangled or GitLab. For GitLab, the &lt;a href="https://forgejo.org/docs/latest/user/repo-mirror/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Forgejo docs&lt;/a&gt; say to use &lt;code&gt;oauth2&lt;/code&gt; as the username and a token with the &lt;code&gt;write_repository&lt;/code&gt; scope. SSH push mirrors generate an Ed25519 key that you add as a deploy key on the target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two warnings from those docs: push mirrors are forced and overwrite the remote, and LFS over SSH isn't implemented, so an SSH push mirror won't carry your LFS objects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Option 4: Tangled, the federated forge on ATProto
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tangled is built on the AT Protocol, the same protocol Bluesky runs on. Repos live on "knots," which you can host yourself or leave on tangled.org. CI runs on "spindles." The October 30 release added knot2, a Rust rewrite of the knot server, and moved issues, PRs, comments, reactions and labels onto the knot as collaborative objects, each one "just a chain of Git objects in the repo itself." That matters for mirroring: if your knot holds the issues too, your backup carries the issue and PR discussion along with the commits. The blog's pitch is "Whatever you choose, you can still talk to everyone else."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pushing to it is plain SSH. Add your key under Settings &amp;gt; Keys, then:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight ssh"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;Host&lt;/span&gt; tangled.org
&lt;span class="k"&gt;Hostname&lt;/span&gt; tangled.org
&lt;span class="k"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt; git
&lt;span class="k"&gt;IdentityFile&lt;/span&gt; ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
&lt;span class="k"&gt;AddressFamily&lt;/span&gt; inet
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For a backup, the gaps count for more than the features:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Private repos aren't available yet. A co-founder said on HN they're rolling out "in the following couple weeks," and another team member said they have a working demo. Until they ship, Tangled only works for public code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;HTTPS push and clone came up because people want knots behind Cloudflare. A team member replied that HTTPS push already works but isn't documented yet, so expect to figure it out yourself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On Windows, one commenter couldn't clone a repo with a colon in a filename. That's a Windows limit, not a Tangled bug, but it'll bite you the same way.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Self-hosted knot operators have to upgrade to v1.20.0 for the new release.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most of &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50305573" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;that thread&lt;/a&gt; is an argument about the protocol. One camp says ATProto "heavily lends itself to centralization." The other camp says relays are cheap and that ForgeFed, the ActivityPub approach Forgejo was working on, has been "in limbo forever." I lean toward Tangled here, but for a reason other than the protocol. A federated forge that ships stacked PRs today is more useful than a theoretically better protocol that hasn't shipped. Still, I wouldn't make a forge without private repos my only backup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The CI fallback: Forgejo Actions, Woodpecker or Tangled spindles
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A mirror with no CI is a read-only archive. You don't need to port your whole pipeline, just enough to run tests and cut a release by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where it runs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Config&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Notes&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Forgejo Actions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your runner, or Codeberg's &lt;a href="https://docs.codeberg.org/ci/actions/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;limited hosted Actions&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;.forgejo/workflows/*.yml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Designed to feel familiar to GitHub Actions users, though not compatible; disabled by default per repo on Codeberg&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Woodpecker CI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Codeberg's hosted instance (request access) or self-hosted&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;.woodpecker.yml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What Codeberg's docs recommend for hosted CI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tangled spindles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Self-hosted runner, or third-party CI via &lt;a href="https://tangled.org/mitchellh.com/tack" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tack&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;.tangled/workflows/*.yml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nixery engine, deps from Nixpkgs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A minimal spindle workflow looks like this. The &lt;a href="https://tangled.org/j3s.sh/core/blob/ci/docs/spindle/pipeline.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pipeline docs&lt;/a&gt; list &lt;code&gt;push&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;pull_request&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;manual&lt;/code&gt; as events, and &lt;code&gt;nixery&lt;/code&gt; as the only engine for now:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;push"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pull_request"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;branch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;main"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;engine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;nixery"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;nixpkgs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;go&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;go&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;./..."&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The useful part of the Forgejo runner is that it connects outbound to the forge, so a box behind NAT works. Codeberg's docs say it "does not need a public IP address." An old laptop in a closet is enough to unblock a hotfix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The one-hour checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add the &lt;code&gt;ghdown&lt;/code&gt; alias, or a bookmark to &lt;code&gt;https://www.githubstatus.com/api/v2/summary.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create empty repos on Codeberg and/or Tangled.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add them as extra push URLs on &lt;code&gt;origin&lt;/code&gt;, or set up a pull mirror on your own Forgejo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make sure the mirror doesn't depend on GitHub Actions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Copy your test job to one fallback CI and run it once, so you know it works before you need it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write down which secrets the release job needs and where they'd live on the backup forge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not leaving GitHub. It's still where people find your project, and it will probably stay that way. I just want "GitHub is down" to mean I push somewhere else for 90 minutes and keep working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So: do you keep a mirror of your repos anywhere besides GitHub, or are you all in? And if you've tried Tangled's ATProto setup, is it federation or a nicer-looking single point of failure?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.githubstatus.com/incidents/pwxsjgnvrk6b" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Status: Incident with Actions, Pages, and Webhooks&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50307723" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HN discussion&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.githubstatus.com/api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Status API&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://tangled.org/blog/forge/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tangled: The federated code forge&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50305573" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HN discussion&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://tangled.org/j3s.sh/core/blob/ci/docs/spindle/pipeline.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tangled spindle pipeline docs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://forgejo.org/docs/latest/user/repo-mirror/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Forgejo: Repository mirrors&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://docs.codeberg.org/getting-started/faq/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Codeberg FAQ&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.codeberg.org/ci/actions/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Codeberg: Forgejo Actions&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you run Claude Desktop with Model Context Protocol servers under heavy production loads, you hit a silent wall. Operations freeze, background jobs drop, and connections fail with cryptic errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The issue is not your database or server logic. It is a hardcoded default inside Claude Desktop. The application enforces a strict 30,000 millisecond timeout for startup handshakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an MCP server takes longer than 30 seconds to boot, initialize dependencies, or parse configuration files, Claude terminates the process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the technical walkthrough, architectural breakdown, and code fixes to eliminate these connection timeouts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 30,000ms Bottleneck: Why Claude MCP Drops Long-Running Connections
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffv8yzjxdgxkmk8upt071.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffv8yzjxdgxkmk8upt071.webp" alt=" " width="800" height="451"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
MCP does not run like standard HTTP APIs. It relies on persistent STDIO or SSE channels to pass tools and prompts between the client and local processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Default Handshake Limits in Claude Desktop
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Desktop allocates 30 seconds for the initial connection handshake. During this window, your server must initialize stdout and stdin channels, respond to the initialize JSON-RPC method call, and return available capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your script performs database connection pooling, fetches remote secrets, or loads heavy libraries during startup, it hits the timeout limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step-by-Step Fix: Overriding Default Timeout Settings
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Method 1: Modifying Environment Variables in claude_desktop_config.json
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6d7jdj8dq6rvqfwi2kle.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6d7jdj8dq6rvqfwi2kle.webp" alt=" " width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
To override connection timeout thresholds, pass explicit environment variables directly within your JSON configuration file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open your claude_desktop_config.json file located at APPDATA\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json on Windows or Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json on macOS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add scoped server timeouts directly inside the env object:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;{&lt;br&gt;
"mcpServers": {&lt;br&gt;
"production-data-ingest": {&lt;br&gt;
"command": "node",&lt;br&gt;
"args": ["C:/mcp-servers/data-ingest/dist/server.js"],&lt;br&gt;
"env": {&lt;br&gt;
"MCP_TIMEOUT_MS": "120000",&lt;br&gt;
"NODE_OPTIONS": "--max-old-space-size=4096"&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Method 2: Adjusting Client-Side Call Parameters in Python
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you invoke MCP servers programmatically using Python, the default ClientSession timeout drops long-running tool executions. Pass explicit timedelta values to read_timeout_seconds when initializing the session.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import asyncio&lt;br&gt;
from datetime import timedelta&lt;br&gt;
from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters&lt;br&gt;
from mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;server_params = StdioServerParameters(&lt;br&gt;
command="python",&lt;br&gt;
args=["heavy_worker.py"]&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;async def run_pipeline():&lt;br&gt;
async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):&lt;br&gt;
async with ClientSession(&lt;br&gt;
read, &lt;br&gt;
write, &lt;br&gt;
read_timeout_seconds=timedelta(seconds=120)&lt;br&gt;
) as session:&lt;br&gt;
await session.initialize()&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;        result = await session.call_tool(
"process_large_dataset",
arguments={"batch_size": 5000},
read_timeout_seconds=timedelta(seconds=180)
)
print(result)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;if &lt;strong&gt;name&lt;/strong&gt; == "&lt;strong&gt;main&lt;/strong&gt;":&lt;br&gt;
asyncio.run(run_pipeline())&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Advanced Engineering Fixes for Production Pipelines
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Implementing Asynchronous Handshakes and Exponential Backoff
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In distributed production setups, immediate retries cause thundering herd problems when an MCP server experiences latency. Implement asynchronous backoff loops with random jitter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import asyncio&lt;br&gt;
import random&lt;br&gt;
import logging&lt;br&gt;
from datetime import timedelta&lt;br&gt;
from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters&lt;br&gt;
from mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client&lt;br&gt;
from mcp.shared.exceptions import McpError&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)&lt;br&gt;
logger = logging.getLogger("mcp-resilience")&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;async def connect_with_retry(max_retries=5, base_delay=2.0):&lt;br&gt;
server_params = StdioServerParameters(&lt;br&gt;
command="node", &lt;br&gt;
args=["dist/index.js"]&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;for attempt in range(max_retries):&lt;br&gt;
try:&lt;br&gt;
async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):&lt;br&gt;
async with ClientSession(&lt;br&gt;
read, &lt;br&gt;
write, &lt;br&gt;
read_timeout_seconds=timedelta(seconds=90)&lt;br&gt;
) as session:&lt;br&gt;
await session.initialize()&lt;br&gt;
logger.info("Successfully established MCP session.")&lt;br&gt;
return session&lt;br&gt;
except (McpError, ConnectionError, TimeoutError) as err:&lt;br&gt;
if attempt == max_retries - 1:&lt;br&gt;
logger.error("Max connection attempts reached.")&lt;br&gt;
raise err
&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;    sleep_duration = (base_delay ** attempt) + random.uniform(0.1, 0.5)
logger.warning(f"Attempt failed: {err}. Retrying in {sleep_duration:.2f}s...")
await asyncio.sleep(sleep_duration)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
Final Technical Takeaways&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Startup handshakes and tool executions fail for different architectural reasons. Fix the startup routine first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never run database schema migrations or heavy library imports during the initial process boot sequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Always use absolute paths when targeting runtimes like node or python on Windows platforms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Implement client-side exponential backoff loops to recover from transient connection drops gracefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🚹Author Bio:&lt;br&gt;
The Dollar Craft is an independent AI research publication documenting B2B automation architectures, private local RAG deployments, and no-code data infrastructure for legal and financial firms across North America and Europe. We specialize in analyzing compliance-first AI integrations, self-hosted n8n workflows, and data-backed systems for modern enterprises.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;⚖️&amp;gt; &lt;strong&gt;Disclaimer:&lt;/strong&gt; This article is for educational and informational purposes only. The views expressed are my own and do not reflect the official stance of any vendor or tool mentioned. Test all configurations and scripts in a staging environment before running them in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Supply chain security sounds like a problem for organisations with a security function, and the framing is misleading. A small team running a handful of services typically depends on hundreds of packages it has never read, pulled at build time from registries it does not control, executing installation scripts on machines that hold its deployment credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exposure is not proportional to company size. It is proportional to dependency count and build automation, and small teams often have more of the first and less scrutiny of the second than the organisations that worry about this publicly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The uncomfortable arithmetic:&lt;/strong&gt; your application has perhaps a dozen direct dependencies and several hundred transitive ones. You chose the dozen. You did not choose the rest, you have not read any of them, and any one of them running an install script has the same access as your build process. That is the actual attack surface, and it is not visible from the dependency list you wrote.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Supply Chain Security Starts at Install Time
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dangerous moment is usually not running the code. It is installing it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Package managers permit scripts to execute during installation, which means a compromised or malicious package runs with the privileges of whoever ran the install. On a developer machine that is their credentials. In a build pipeline it is the deployment keys, which is worse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three attack shapes account for most real incidents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Typosquatting.&lt;/strong&gt; A package with a name one character from a popular one, published and waiting for a mistyped install command. Cheap to execute and effective at scale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Account compromise of a legitimate package.&lt;/strong&gt; A widely used package whose maintainer's credentials were stolen, updated with something harmful. This is the hardest to defend against because nothing about the name or the download count looks wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dependency confusion.&lt;/strong&gt; A public package published under the name of one of your internal ones, where a misconfigured resolver prefers the public registry. Entirely a configuration problem and entirely preventable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these require anyone to target you specifically, which is the point. They are opportunistic and they scale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Controls That Repay the Effort at Small Scale
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Commit the lockfile and install from it.&lt;/strong&gt; A lockfile pins exact versions and their hashes. Installing from the manifest instead resolves fresh versions at build time, which means your build is not reproducible and an upstream change reaches production without review. Use the install command that fails when the lockfile disagrees rather than the one that updates it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disable install scripts where you can.&lt;/strong&gt; Many ecosystems support this, and most packages do not need them. It is the single largest reduction in exposure available, and the packages that break are worth knowing about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Automate vulnerability alerts, then triage.&lt;/strong&gt; Scanners produce more findings than a small team can act on, and the failure mode is not missing alerts, it is ignoring all of them because most are irrelevant. Filter to what is reachable from your code and act on that. A queue nobody reads is worse than no queue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pin your build tooling too.&lt;/strong&gt; The container image, the runtime version and the action or plugin your pipeline calls are all dependencies, and referencing a mutable tag means your build changes without you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Separate build credentials from everything else.&lt;/strong&gt; A pipeline that can deploy should not hold credentials that can do anything more, and a compromised build should not be able to reach production data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What an SBOM Does and Does Not Tell You
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A software bill of materials lists what is in your application, and its value is answering one question fast: are we affected by this?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That question used to take days. When a widely used component turns out to be vulnerable, an organisation with a current inventory answers in minutes and one without it spends a week searching. That difference is the whole argument, and it is why &lt;a href="https://www.cisa.gov/sbom" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SBOM adoption is being pushed&lt;/a&gt; as baseline practice rather than as an advanced control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it does not do is make you secure. An inventory is a list, not a defence. It does not tell you whether a listed vulnerability is reachable in your code, whether it matters in your configuration, or whether anything has been compromised. Teams that generate one and file it have added a document rather than a control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate it from the build, so it describes what was actually shipped rather than what the manifest claimed. Keep the one matching each release. And accept that its value is entirely in the speed of answering a question you will be asked under pressure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where Small Teams Actually Get Hurt
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not usually by an exotic package compromise. By ordinary things.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Secrets in the repository&lt;/strong&gt;, which remain in history after deletion and are found by automated scanners within minutes of a push. &lt;strong&gt;A dependency three years out of date&lt;/strong&gt; with a published exploit and no upgrade path, because upgrading was deferred until it became a project. &lt;strong&gt;Build pipelines with excessive permissions&lt;/strong&gt;, where a pull request from a fork can run with access it should not have. &lt;strong&gt;Nobody watching&lt;/strong&gt;, so a compromise persists because no alert reaches a person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The unglamorous conclusion is that keeping dependencies current is most of supply chain security in practice. The &lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/posts/software-maintenance-cost/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;software maintenance cost&lt;/a&gt; that teams treat as optional is the same work, and deferring it converts a routine upgrade into an emergency with an exploit attached.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Proportionate Starting Point
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small team, in order: get secrets out of the repository and rotate anything exposed. Commit lockfiles and install from them strictly. Turn on dependency alerts and triage weekly rather than continuously. Pin build tooling to immutable references. Reduce pipeline permissions to the minimum. Generate an SBOM per release once the rest is in place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That sequence covers the realistic threats without a security function, and each step is hours rather than weeks. The controls beyond it, provenance attestation, reproducible builds, signed artefacts, are genuinely valuable and belong to a stage where the basics are already stable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mecanik reviews and hardens build pipelines as part of our &lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/services/application-security/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;application security&lt;/a&gt; work. The finding is almost never a sophisticated compromise. It is a token in a repository and a dependency nobody has updated since it was chosen.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Related reading&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/posts/password-storage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Password Storage: What to Use in 2026&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/posts/wordpress-security-hardening-checklist-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WordPress Security Hardening Checklist for 2026&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/posts/uptime-sla/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Uptime SLAs That Mean Something&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://mecanik.dev/en/posts/api-security-protect-public-api/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API Security: How to Protect a Public API in 2026&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why is installing packages the risky moment?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Because package managers allow scripts to run during installation, with the privileges of whoever ran the install. On a developer machine that means their credentials; in a build pipeline it means deployment keys. The code does not have to be executed by your application to do damage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is dependency confusion?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
An attack where a public package is published under the name of one of your internal packages, and a misconfigured resolver prefers the public registry over your private one. It requires nobody to target you specifically and is entirely preventable through resolver configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What does an SBOM actually give you?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
A fast answer to one question: are we affected by this? With a current inventory that takes minutes; without one it takes a week of searching. It does not tell you whether a listed vulnerability is reachable in your code or whether anything has been compromised, so an SBOM that is generated and filed is a document rather than a control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Should I commit my lockfile?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes, and install from it strictly using the command that fails when the lockfile disagrees rather than the one that updates it. Installing from the manifest resolves fresh versions at build time, which makes builds unreproducible and lets an upstream change reach production without any review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where should a small team start?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Remove secrets from the repository and rotate anything exposed, commit lockfiles and install strictly, enable dependency alerts and triage them weekly, pin build tooling to immutable references, reduce pipeline permissions, then generate an SBOM per release. Each step is hours rather than weeks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A public holiday is a legal fact: the law says the office is closed. A religious observance is something else. Ramadan, Passover or Lunar New Year don't close anything in Lisbon, but a lot of people still plan around them: shift rotas, delivery windows, a marketing calendar, a meeting you shouldn't put on Yom Kippur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So hldays has a second layer next to holidays: observances. There are 46 of them across six traditions (Western Christian, Orthodox Christian, Jewish, Islamic, Chinese and Buddhist), served by &lt;code&gt;/v1/observances&lt;/code&gt; and attached to the date endpoints on paid plans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added them for exactly that kind of scheduling. The API already knew when offices close, and it felt incomplete not to know when a lot of people are busy anyway.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post is mostly about the calendars, because that's where the interesting problems were.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Data first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each tradition is one YAML file in &lt;code&gt;data/observances/&lt;/code&gt;. An observance looks a lot like a holiday rule, with three extra ideas: a span (&lt;code&gt;days&lt;/code&gt; or an &lt;code&gt;end&lt;/code&gt; rule), an evening start (&lt;code&gt;eve&lt;/code&gt;), and whether the date is a calculation that might be off (&lt;code&gt;estimated&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;passover&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Passover"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;he&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;פסח"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;rule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;hebrew&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;Nisan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;eve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;diaspora,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Israel."&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rule kinds are the same ones the holiday engine uses (&lt;code&gt;src/core/dates.ts&lt;/code&gt;), so an observance can be a fixed date, an offset from Easter, an offset from Orthodox Easter, or a day in another calendar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Easter: the one with an algorithm
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Western Easter is the anonymous Gregorian algorithm, straight from the textbook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** Gregorian Easter Sunday (anonymous Gregorian algorithm). */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;easterSunday&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;19&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ... eleven more lines of integer arithmetic&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;114&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;114&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;UTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Everything movable in the Western calendar is an offset from it: Ash Wednesday is &lt;code&gt;{ easter: -46 }&lt;/code&gt;, Pentecost &lt;code&gt;{ easter: 49 }&lt;/code&gt;. The same function already drives holidays like Good Friday and Corpus Christi in the national data, so observances got it for free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Orthodox Easter: same idea, different calendar
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Orthodox churches compute Easter on the Julian calendar. The code uses Meeus' Julian algorithm and then shifts the result onto the Gregorian calendar:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** Orthodox Easter Sunday as a Gregorian date (Meeus' Julian algorithm + Julian→Gregorian shift; valid 1900–2099). */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;orthodoxEasterSunday&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;19&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;19&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;34&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;114&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;114&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;shift&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 13 days in 1900–2099&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;addDays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;UTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;shift&lt;/code&gt; line is the bit worth noticing: the gap between the two calendars grows by a day in most century years, and it's 13 days for the whole range hldays supports (2016 to 2099). The test includes the year both Easters coincide, which is a good sanity check:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;formatDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;orthodoxEasterSunday&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2025-04-20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// same day as Western Easter&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;formatDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;orthodoxEasterSunday&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-04-12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expandRule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;orthodox_easter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;48&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-02-23&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Greek Clean Monday&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The YAML also notes a split inside Orthodoxy: Julian-calendar churches celebrate Christmas on 7 January, while Revised Julian churches (Greek, Romanian, Bulgarian) use 25 December but share the Orthodox Easter cycle. That's a note on the record, not two records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Hebrew and Islamic dates: let Intl do it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't want to ship conversion tables for every calendar. JavaScript's &lt;code&gt;Intl.DateTimeFormat&lt;/code&gt; already knows the Hebrew and Islamic calendars (through ICU), so the engine builds an index for a Gregorian year by formatting every day of it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Intl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DateTimeFormat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`en-u-ca-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeZone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;UTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;numeric&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;numeric&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Intl silently falls back to Gregorian when a runtime lacks the calendar; wrong dates are worse than an error.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolvedOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Intl calendar &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; is not supported`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That second line is my favourite in the file. If a runtime doesn't support a calendar, &lt;code&gt;Intl&lt;/code&gt; doesn't throw: it quietly formats Gregorian dates instead. Without the check, a runtime without full ICU would treat Islamic month 10, day 1 as 1 October and put Eid al-Fitr there. I checked that the output on Cloudflare Workers matches Node before relying on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Hebrew calendar had one naming trap. In leap years there are two Adars, and Intl calls the second one "Adar II". Purim is in Adar II in leap years, so the index stores "Adar II" under "Adar":&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^Adar II$/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Adar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// "Adar" is Adar II in leap years (Purim)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expandRule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;purim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2024-03-24&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expandRule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;purim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2025-03-14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Islamic dates are estimates, and the API says so
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Islamic calendar is lunar, and in many countries a month starts when the new moon is actually &lt;em&gt;sighted&lt;/em&gt;. No algorithm can know that in advance. hldays calculates with the Umm al-Qura calendar (Saudi Arabia's), and the YAML says up front what that means:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Islamic observances, calculated with the Umm al-Qura calendar (Saudi Arabia). Many countries start months on
# a local moon sighting, so the observed date can differ by a day: every entry is marked estimated.

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every Islamic observance has &lt;code&gt;estimated: true&lt;/code&gt;, and it comes through in the response so clients can show "around" instead of a firm date. Here's Eid al-Fitr on a business-day call (paid plan, run locally):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"jurisdiction"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PT-M-1106"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lisboa"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"date"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-03-20"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"business_day"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"holidays"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"observances"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"observance_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"eid-al-fitr"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tradition"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"islamic"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"en"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Eid al-Fitr"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"ar"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"عيد الفطر"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"start"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-03-20"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"end"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-03-22"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"starts_previous_evening"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"estimated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;

&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;starts_previous_evening&lt;/code&gt; comes from &lt;code&gt;eve: true&lt;/code&gt;: Jewish and Islamic days begin at sunset, so Eid "on" 20 March starts on the evening of the 19th.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lunar year is about 11 days shorter than a Gregorian one, which breaks a quiet assumption: that a date happens once a year. Eid al-Fitr happens twice in 2033. The rule expander returns a list for that reason, and the test makes it explicit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The Islamic year is ~11 days shorter, so some Gregorian years hold the same date twice&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expandRule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;eid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2033&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toHaveLength&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Spans that cross New Year
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ramadan doesn't have a fixed length: it's 29 or 30 days. So instead of &lt;code&gt;days&lt;/code&gt;, it has an &lt;code&gt;end&lt;/code&gt; rule: the day before 1 Shawwal.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ramadan&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;rule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;islamic-umalqura&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;islamic-umalqura&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;-1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Around 2030 Ramadan starts in December and ends in January. Asking "what's observed on 5 January 2031?" has to find a span that &lt;em&gt;started the previous year&lt;/em&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;Observances.between&lt;/code&gt; always looks one year back:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A span can start in the previous Gregorian year (Ramadan across New Year).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2031-01-05&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;observance_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ramadan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2030-12-26&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2031-01-23&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;estimated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


The Chinese calendar: where Intl was wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I started with Intl for the Chinese calendar too. Then I compared it with the Hong Kong Observatory's published tables and found that ICU gets 5 of the 1039 month starts between 2016 and 2099 wrong by a day. Two of them are Chinese New Year: 2027 and 2030.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can see it in Node (ICU 78.3):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;2027-02-06 12/30 &amp;lt;- ICU: last day of the 12th month
2027-02-07 1/1 &amp;lt;- ICU: New Year
2030-02-02 1/1 &amp;lt;- ICU: New Year

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The HKO tables say 6 February 2027 and 3 February 2030. Being a day off on the biggest date in the calendar wasn't acceptable, so for the Chinese calendar only, hldays uses HKO's data. &lt;code&gt;scripts/import-chinese-calendar.ts&lt;/code&gt; downloads one text file per year and keeps just the month starts:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;(\d{4})\/(\d{1,2})\/(\d{1,2})\s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;(\d{1,2})(?:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;st|nd|rd|th&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt; Lunar Month/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;line&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// HKO repeats the month number for a leap month&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;leap&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;starts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;padStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;padStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;leap&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The result is &lt;code&gt;src/core/chinese-months.json&lt;/code&gt;: 1077 entries like &lt;code&gt;"2014-01-31 1"&lt;/code&gt;, from 2014 to 2100. Leap months are marked &lt;code&gt;L&lt;/code&gt; and never match a rule, so "2nd month, day 1" in 2023 is the regular second month and not the leap one that follows it. The tests check the exact days ICU gets wrong:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2027 and 2030 are days ICU's Chinese calendar gets wrong; the engine uses the HKO table&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2027&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2030&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;flatMap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expandRule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lny&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2025-01-29&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-02-17&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2027-02-06&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2030-02-03&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The table starts in 2014, not 2016, and git history explains why. The first version guarded the range strictly and rejected 2014, but &lt;code&gt;/v1/observances?year=2016&lt;/code&gt; needs it: observances always look one year back (for spans like Ramadan), and Lunar New Year's Eve is "1st month, day 1, offset -1", where an offset makes the engine expand the neighbouring years too. 2016 reaches 2015, and 2015 reaches 2014. A test now asks for the first and last day of the supported range, so that can't come back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Observances never affect business days
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This was a design rule from the first commit of the feature: observances are information, not days off. The business-day code doesn't read them at all. &lt;code&gt;isBusinessDay&lt;/code&gt; looks at the weekend and at holidays, and observances are attached to the response afterwards:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;calendar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isBusinessDay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;types&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;municipal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jurisdiction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;holidays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;withSources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;holidays&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observancesOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Where an observance &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; a day off, like Good Friday in Portugal, it's also a holiday in that country's data, with a source. The observance list just tells you it's Good Friday; the holiday list tells you the office is closed. Mixing the two would mean Eid closes Lisbon, which it doesn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Plans and limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Observances are on the paid plans only. On the free plan, &lt;code&gt;/v1/observances&lt;/code&gt; returns 403 &lt;code&gt;paid_plan_required&lt;/code&gt;, and the date endpoints return &lt;code&gt;observances: []&lt;/code&gt; with an &lt;code&gt;observances_notice&lt;/code&gt; that says how many were hidden, so a free client still knows something is there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What isn't covered yet:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Theravada Vesak.&lt;/strong&gt; The Buddhist file has only the East Asian Buddha's Birthday; Vesak needs a lunar calendar the engine doesn't support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hindu and Sikh calendars.&lt;/strong&gt; Nothing yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Regional variants.&lt;/strong&gt; Jewish festivals use diaspora lengths (Israel's are in &lt;code&gt;notes&lt;/code&gt;), Shia dates for Mawlid are a note, and Korea and Vietnam can differ from China by a day where their time zone does.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which calendar would you want next: Hindu, Vesak, or something else? Tell me in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next in the series: making an API agent-friendly with MCP, and designing tools for Claude and other agents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://hldays.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hldays.dev&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://hldays.dev/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Docs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Corevia is evolving beyond a traditional PHP framework.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is being designed as a &lt;strong&gt;multi-runtime application framework&lt;/strong&gt; where PHP can remain the core application layer while Python, JavaScript, React, Dart and Flutter can participate through defined contracts, SDKs and services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is not to replace every technology with PHP.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is to let different technologies do what they are best at — without turning the application into an integration mess.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What Corevia already focuses on
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• Modular Actions, Flows and Events&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Durable workflows with retries, pause/resume and compensation&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Runtime idempotency and failure isolation&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Realtime communication through WebSockets&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• PHP ↔ Python interoperability&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Cross-runtime application architecture&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• HTTP routing and API development&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Database adapters&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• CLI tooling and runtime inspection&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Lightweight PHP 8.2+ core&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Architecture designed for custom web applications, APIs, workers and business systems  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Multi-platform direction
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Corevia is also being expanded to make frontend and application clients easier to integrate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The direction includes support and SDK patterns for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• &lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt; — web applications and dashboards&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• &lt;strong&gt;JavaScript / TypeScript&lt;/strong&gt; — browser and application clients&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• &lt;strong&gt;Dart&lt;/strong&gt; — cross-platform application integrations&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• &lt;strong&gt;Flutter&lt;/strong&gt; — mobile, desktop and cross-platform clients&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• &lt;strong&gt;Python&lt;/strong&gt; — workers, automation, AI services and specialized processing  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of tightly coupling these runtimes, Corevia is moving toward explicit contracts that define how applications communicate with the framework.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


AI is also becoming an important part of Corevia
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI support in Corevia is not intended to mean simply adding a chatbot endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The framework architecture is being designed so AI can participate as another application capability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That opens the door for things such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• Local and hosted LLM integrations&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Python-based AI workers&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• AI-powered automation flows&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Background inference jobs&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• AI agents operating through controlled application actions&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Retrieval and knowledge systems&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Structured tool execution&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• AI-assisted CRM and business workflows&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Chatbots and auto-repliers&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Classification, extraction and summarization services&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Local/private AI deployments&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• Multi-model provider adapters  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part is &lt;strong&gt;control&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI services should interact with the application through defined actions, permissions and contracts rather than receiving unrestricted access to the system.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h1&gt;


Corevia V3 — Under Development
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;V3 has not been publicly released yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A significant portion of the roadmap has already been implemented internally, while development is continuing on the remaining architectural pieces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Current V3 work includes stronger framework/application separation, safer upgrades, starter kits, application foundations, background processing, improved SDKs, AI-oriented capabilities and better developer tooling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Six major architectural areas remain under active development:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Deterministic Module Composition
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Making module registration, dependency resolution and boot order predictable and reproducible across environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Runtime Profiles / Capability Presets
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications should be able to load only what they need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Web&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;API&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;CLI&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Worker&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Realtime&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;AI&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Hybrid&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;instead of booting every framework capability for every process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Explicit Request &amp;amp; Worker Lifecycle
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clear lifecycle boundaries for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• HTTP requests&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• CLI commands&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• background workers&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• realtime processes&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• long-running services&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• AI workers  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This becomes especially important when PHP and Python services operate together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Machine-Enforced Architecture Boundaries
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architectural rules should not exist only in documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Corevia should be able to detect and prevent invalid dependencies, module violations and incorrect application structure automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Reproducible Build &amp;amp; Configuration Fingerprints
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Corevia application should be able to identify exactly which framework configuration, modules and runtime capabilities produced a particular deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This should make development, staging and production environments easier to compare and reproduce.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


6. Cross-Runtime Contract Manifest
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most important V3 pieces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A formal contract layer for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PHP&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Python&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
JavaScript&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
React&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Dart&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Flutter&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
and future runtimes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The manifest will define things such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• available actions&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• payload contracts&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• capabilities&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• runtime requirements&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• validation rules&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• version compatibility&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
• remote-service discovery  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The objective is to make cross-runtime development predictable rather than relying on loosely documented API agreements.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Corevia V3 is not about adding features just to make the framework larger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is about making the framework:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;More deterministic.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
More modular.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
More portable.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
More AI-ready.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
More cross-platform.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
And safer to scale across multiple runtimes.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The underlying philosophy remains simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep simple applications simple — while giving complex systems room to grow.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


PHP #Python #Flutter #React #Dart #ArtificialIntelligence #SoftwareEngineering #OpenSource #Framework
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In modern mobile-first web development, reducing conversion friction on landing pages is the fastest lever to increase inbound lead velocity. While desktop users can click on an inline WhatsApp link to open WhatsApp Web, mobile and cross-device visitors benefit significantly from dynamic, scannable QR codes rendered directly on screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unlike raster image formats (PNG, JPEG) which blur upon zooming or increase HTTP payload sizes, generating vector SVG QR codes client-side ensures crisp rendering on retina displays with zero external image requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will build a lightweight, responsive WhatsApp QR code component in React and TypeScript that formats dynamic click-to-chat deep links.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Understanding the WhatsApp Deep Link Protocol
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WhatsApp supports universal deep links formatted as:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;https://wa.me/&amp;lt;PhoneNumber&amp;gt;?text=&amp;lt;EncodedMessage&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Key formatting requirements:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;E.164 Format&lt;/strong&gt;: The phone number must include the international country code without plus signs, dashes, or leading zeros (e.g., &lt;code&gt;15551234567&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;URL Encoding&lt;/strong&gt;: The message string must be properly URI-encoded using &lt;code&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/code&gt; to handle emojis, spaces, and punctuation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Step 1: Installing the Lightweight QR Code Generator
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We will use &lt;code&gt;qrcode&lt;/code&gt; which supports zero-dependency SVG string generation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;qrcode
npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--save-dev&lt;/span&gt; @types/qrcode
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h3&gt;


Step 2: Creating the Responsive React QR Component
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the complete implementation of our WhatsApp QR component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;QRCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;qrcode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;WhatsAppQRProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;phoneNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// e.g. "15551234567"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;defaultMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;WhatsAppQRCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;FC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;WhatsAppQRProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;phoneNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;defaultMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;220&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cleanPhone&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;phoneNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;[^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;0-9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;encodedText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;defaultMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;waUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`https://wa.me/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cleanPhone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;?text=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;encodedText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;svgString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setSvgString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;QRCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;waUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#128C7E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// WhatsApp Brand Green&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;light&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#FFFFFF&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setSvgString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;waUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inline-block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;textAlign&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;center&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dangerouslySetInnerHTML&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;__html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;svgString&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;0 auto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;waUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;_blank&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;noopener noreferrer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inline-block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;marginTop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;8px 16px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;backgroundColor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#25D366&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#fff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;borderRadius&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;textDecoration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fontWeight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;600&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Chat&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;WhatsApp&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h3&gt;


Enhancing Conversion Tracking and Lead Routing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To measure campaign effectiveness, avoid hardcoding static numbers in your production client bundle. Instead:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use UTM campaign tracking parameters within the pre-filled message string so your CRM can attribute inbound chats to specific ads or blog posts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For non-technical marketing teams, you can generate instant, trackable QR links using the &lt;a href="https://aisoule.com/tools/link-generator" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Free WhatsApp Link Generator&lt;/a&gt; on AI Soul.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Once prospects scan the code and start chatting, route them into a multi-agent shared workspace with &lt;a href="https://aisoule.com/features/conversational-crm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Conversational CRM Features&lt;/a&gt; on &lt;a href="https://aisoule.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Soul&lt;/a&gt; and deploy automated &lt;a href="https://aisoule.com/features/ai-chatbot" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Chatbot Solutions&lt;/a&gt; to qualify inquiries immediately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Summary
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generate QR codes client-side in SVG format for zero layout shift and retina crispness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sanitize phone numbers to pure numeric E.164 strings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pair QR codes with direct one-tap links to serve both desktop and mobile users seamlessly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confession: bundle analyzers are the tool I know I should open more often and mostly don't. The usual finding is something small and slightly embarrassing, like a date library imported for one &lt;code&gt;format()&lt;/code&gt; call and sitting in the initial chunk of a page with no dates on it. You fix it, feel smug for a day, and forget to check again until someone complains about load time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So when the Next.js team published &lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/blog/ship-less-javascript-with-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ship less JavaScript in Next.js with your AI agent&lt;/a&gt; on 28 October, I read it twice. Short version for the impatient: Next.js 16.4 adds an agent skill and a better &lt;code&gt;next analyze&lt;/code&gt;, and the most useful idea in the post is a boring one about imports that quietly undo each other. I haven't run the skill on a production app yet, so this isn't a review. It's what I would check first, and why I'm more interested in the analyzer than in the agent bolted onto it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The lazy import that never deferred anything
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the example from the Next.js post, nearly verbatim in spirit. You have a heavy component and you want it out of the initial bundle, so you wrap it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// HeavyComponentLoader.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lazy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;HeavyComponentLoader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;lazy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./HeavyComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That looks right. React's &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/lazy" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;lazy&lt;/a&gt; documentation says the code for a lazy component isn't loaded until it first renders, and the Next.js &lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/lazy-loading" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;lazy loading guide&lt;/a&gt; says much the same about &lt;code&gt;next/dynamic&lt;/code&gt;. Both are true. The catch is that the bundler works on the whole module graph, not on your intentions. If any other module in the same bundle does this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Toolbar.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;HeavyComponent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./HeavyComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;then &lt;code&gt;HeavyComponent&lt;/code&gt; is part of the initial chunk anyway, and your lazy wrapper becomes a decoration. One static import, three folders away, written by someone who never saw your loader. The bundle doesn't warn you. Lighthouse just gets a little worse and nobody connects it to a pull request from last month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Picture a chart library that is lazy loaded on a reports page, which feels like a good job done. Then someone adds a small summary card to the home page that imports one helper from the same chart file, and the whole library moves into the shared chunk. You'd stare at the lazy call for an hour, because the lazy call is fine. The bug is somewhere else entirely. I haven't been bitten by this exact case on a client project, but it's the shape of bug I'd expect to find in any app with more than one contributor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The old way to find this was to open a treemap, spot the big rectangle, and then grep the codebase for every place that imports it. It works. It's also slow, and you only do it when someone is already complaining about load time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What next analyze gives you without any agent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before the agent part, the human tooling. In 16.1 the team shipped the first Turbopack Bundle Analyzer, and 16.4 adds a few things that matter more than the headline. You run &lt;code&gt;next analyze&lt;/code&gt; in your app directory, and you now land on a route summary that lists your largest client routes. Open one and there's a table you can sort by size, with each source marked as initial load, behind an async boundary, or both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last label is the one I care about. A module marked "Initial + async paths" is exactly the lazy-import bug above: it's loaded lazily from one place and eagerly from another. In the post's illustrative blog app, a search component sits at 18 kB with that label. You can see the problem in one row, without reading a treemap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two more features I'd use right away. The analyzer now saves a snapshot of each analysis automatically, and you can diff two snapshots, which gives you a before and after for any change. The post's example goes from 123 kB to 81 kB on one route, with a per-file table of what moved. And there's a filter for the critical render path, so you can hide async dependencies and look only at what blocks first paint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'll be blunt about one limitation. All of this needs Turbopack, because the analyzer relies on its module graph. If you're on 16 or later, that's the default and you won't think about it. If you pinned webpack for a plugin that doesn't work elsewhere, you stay with the older &lt;a href="https://github.com/webpack-contrib/webpack-bundle-analyzer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;webpack bundle analyzer&lt;/a&gt; and none of this applies to you yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the agent skill adds
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The skill is installed with &lt;code&gt;npx skills add vercel/next.js --skill next-bundle-optimizer&lt;/code&gt; and run with &lt;code&gt;/next-bundle-optimizer&lt;/code&gt;. According to the post, it exports saved analyses as JSON, rebuilds the module and chunk graphs, finds large groups of connected modules, and uses a minimum cut to pick a small set of import boundaries that would defer them. Then it checks those proposals against your source code and how the components are used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The minimum cut is the clever part, and it's a good fit for the problem. "Which handful of imports, if I changed them, would let this whole cluster load later?" is a graph question. It's the question I answer badly by hand, because I follow the first import chain I find and stop. A tool that looks at every chain at once will beat me on that, and I have no pride about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By default it only reports. You have to run &lt;code&gt;/next-bundle-optimizer fix&lt;/code&gt; to have it apply changes, re-run the analysis, and compare snapshots. I like that default. It means the first run on a client codebase is read-only, and I can bill the findings as an audit before anyone touches a file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The numbers in the post are large. Across v0 and vercel.com, the team says agents cut nearly two megabytes of compressed JavaScript, with individual wins like 150 KB from an experimental editor loaded on nearly every page, and 255 KB on the Vercel dashboard from markdown rendering and chart drawing that shipped before the agent panel was even open. Those are self-reported results on Vercel's own apps, tuned by the people who wrote the tool. Treat them as proof the approach works, not as a forecast for your app. A typical agency site has fewer megabytes to find. I'd be happy with three or four wins in the 20 to 80 KB range.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where I would not trust it blindly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deferring code isn't free, and this is where I'd push back on the "let the agent open a pull request" workflow. When you move a 150 KB editor behind a dynamic import, you haven't removed the cost. You've moved it to the moment the user needs the editor. If that moment is a click, the first click now waits on a network request. On a fast connection you won't notice. On a train in poor coverage, the button looks broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So every proposed boundary needs a question: what does the user see while this loads? A spinner, a layout jump, a blank panel? The post says the agent checks how components are used interactively, and I believe it tries. But a model reading source can't feel a 400 ms gap on a mid-range phone. You can.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My review checklist for each agent change, in order:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is the deferred component above the fold or behind an interaction?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does the loading state match the final layout, so nothing shifts?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Did INP get worse on the page, measured on a preview deployment rather than on my laptop?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is the saving big enough to justify the extra moving part?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Vercel team says they verified each fix on a preview deployment before shipping, which is the right habit. Copy it. I'd also keep pull requests one change each, the way the post suggests, because a single combined "optimize bundles" diff is impossible to bisect when INP regresses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are also deciding whether Next.js is the right base for a project in the first place, I went through that trade-off in my &lt;a href="https://abrarqasim.com/blog/nextjs-vs-vite-vinext-1-0-the-use-cache-gap-i-would-check-first" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js vs Vite post&lt;/a&gt;. The bundle tooling above is one more thing that tilts the answer, mostly for teams already on Turbopack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A routine I would run this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need the agent to get most of the value. Here's the sequence I'm going to use on my next client audit, which is the kind of work I describe on &lt;a href="https://abrarqasim.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;my portfolio&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# on a branch, after upgrading to Next.js 16.4&lt;/span&gt;
npx next analyze

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# optional: read-only report from the agent skill&lt;/span&gt;
npx skills add vercel/next.js &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--skill&lt;/span&gt; next-bundle-optimizer
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# then run /next-bundle-optimizer in your agent (no "fix" argument)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# export the graph if you want to poke at it yourself&lt;/span&gt;
npx next analyze &lt;span class="nb"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Start with the route summary and write down the three largest client routes. In each, sort by size and look for two things: any source labeled "Initial + async paths", and any package you can't explain. A date library on a page with no dates counts. Then pick exactly one finding, fix it by hand, take a new snapshot, and read the diff. If the diff shows the number you expected, you understand your bundle. If it doesn't, the import graph is teaching you something, and that's the useful part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only after that would I run the agent in report mode and compare its list with mine. Where we disagree, I want to know why before I apply anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This week, run &lt;code&gt;next analyze&lt;/code&gt; on your biggest route and write down the single largest file in the initial load. If you can't say why it's there, trace its imports until you can.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://abrarqasim.com/blog/nextjs-bundle-analyzer-16-4-the-static-import-that-defeats-lazy-load/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;abrarqasim.com&lt;/a&gt;. I write there about React, PHP, Rust, Go and the AI tooling around them.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every few months, a new "AI research tool" hits the front page of Hacker News or Product Hunt. Almost without exception, they all do the same thing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ingest a PDF.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chunk it into embeddings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Feed the chunks into a vector database.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put a conversational chatbot in front of it that summarizes what the authors said.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the fundamental problem: &lt;strong&gt;Science is not a collection of static text summaries. Science is an evolving, adversarial debate.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A paper published in 2023 demonstrating a 4x inference speedup for 4-bit quantized language models on Apple Silicon is not an absolute truth. It is a finding contingent on specific hardware, quantization algorithms, precision levels, and thermal conditions. When a 2024 paper reports severe degradation under continuous workloads on Qualcomm Snapdragon chips, they aren't necessarily contradicting each other—they're operating in completely different experimental contexts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And when a seminal paper gets retracted or contradicted six months later, your vector database doesn't know. It happily continues serving obsolete claims as ground truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/umeshadabala/PaperTrail" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PaperTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; to fundamentally rethink how software interacts with scientific literature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of another paper summarizer, PaperTrail is an &lt;strong&gt;autonomous, continuously evolving scientific intelligence engine&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, I want to walk through the technical architecture, design philosophy, and lessons learned building PaperTrail with Next.js 14, TypeScript, local Ollama models, and a local-first SQLite/Supabase hybrid storage engine.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Core Proposition
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PaperTrail is built around a single thesis:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;"Discover what research has established, what it challenges, what remains unexplored, and how the scientific evidence changes over time."&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To deliver on this, we established ten non-negotiable architectural capabilities:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Grounded Scientific Claim Extraction&lt;/strong&gt;: Never extract an assertion without an exact, verbatim passage and paper citation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context-Aware Disagreement Investigation&lt;/strong&gt;: Never label two differing findings as a contradiction without examining differences in hardware, precision, quantization bitwidth, context length, or thermal states.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Candidate Research-Gap Discovery&lt;/strong&gt;: Automatically identify blind spots and uninvestigated parameter intersections across literature.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Adversarial 6-Stage Gap Falsification&lt;/strong&gt;: Never present a research gap as valid until an automated red-team agent attempts to aggressively falsify it against counter-evidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Practical Experiment Feasibility &amp;amp; Protocol Planning&lt;/strong&gt;: Convert validated gaps into runnable, resource-evaluated empirical protocols with compute hour calculations and step-by-step checklists.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Continuous Research Tracking &amp;amp; Evidence Evolution&lt;/strong&gt;: Monitor topics and incoming preprints to compute how evidence shifts over time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Preprint-to-Publication Lineage Mapping&lt;/strong&gt;: Trace preprints from arXiv to peer-reviewed venues, isolating shifts in methodology and benchmarks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Retraction &amp;amp; Correction Intelligence&lt;/strong&gt;: When papers are retracted or corrected, flag dependent claims and recalculate conclusions without silently erasing historical evidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessible Dataset Discovery&lt;/strong&gt;: Find benchmark datasets on Hugging Face, Zenodo, and Figshare with verified licenses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cross-Domain Hypothesis Transfer&lt;/strong&gt;: Formulate principled transfer hypotheses connecting primary research with adjacent fields (e.g., neuroscience synaptic pruning $\leftrightarrow$ LLM weight quantization).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


1. Grounded Claim Extraction &amp;amp; Schema Validation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of our strictest engineering rules is: &lt;strong&gt;No hallucinated consensus.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every claim extracted by PaperTrail must adhere to a strict Zod schema. If the model cannot provide the exact supporting passage from the abstract or text, the extraction fails validation and falls back to a deterministic semantic parser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a snippet of our claim validation contract:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ClaimExtractionSchema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;claims&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;claim_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;claim_type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;enum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;FINDING&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;HYPOTHESIS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;METHODOLOGY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;LIMITATION&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;BENCHMARK_RESULT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;THEORETICAL_BOUND&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;enum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Abstract&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Evaluation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Conclusion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;supporting_passage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;page_number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;positive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;optional&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;extraction_confidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;experimental_context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Unspecified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;hardware&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Unspecified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Unspecified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;quantization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;FP16&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;precision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Unspecified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;metrics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variables&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;limitations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([]),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;By extracting both the &lt;strong&gt;claim&lt;/strong&gt; and its &lt;strong&gt;experimental condition matrix&lt;/strong&gt; (hardware, precision, model scale, metrics), we create the foundation for intelligent, context-aware comparison.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


2. Context-Aware Disagreement Investigation: Beyond Binary "Contradictions"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traditional NLP pipelines treat contradiction detection as an NLI (Natural Language Inference) task: &lt;em&gt;Premise vs. Hypothesis $\rightarrow$ Entailment, Neutral, or Contradiction&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In real science, this is catastrophically naive. Consider two papers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Paper A (Lin et al., 2023)&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;"AWQ achieves 3.2x latency speedup with negligible perplexity degradation on 7B LLMs."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Paper B (Smith et al., 2024)&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;"Sustained 4-bit execution throttles after 120 seconds, resulting in a 42% throughput drop on fanless mobile SoCs."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A naive AI system flags these as a direct contradiction: &lt;em&gt;Speedup vs. Throughput Drop&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PaperTrail executes a &lt;strong&gt;Context Comparison Matrix&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ContextComparison&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;similarities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;differences&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;source_val&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;target_val&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;impact_level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;LOW&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;MEDIUM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;HIGH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;possible_explanations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;alternative_explanations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;remaining_uncertainty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;is_genuine_contradiction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When evaluated, PaperTrail identifies:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Independent Variable Difference&lt;/strong&gt;: Paper A evaluated &lt;em&gt;burst inference&lt;/em&gt; under active server cooling; Paper B evaluated &lt;em&gt;sustained execution&lt;/em&gt; under passive ambient mobile cooling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verdict&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;APPARENT_DIVERGENCE_EXPLAINED_BY_CONTEXT&lt;/code&gt; (Confidence: 0.94).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Resulting Research Opportunity&lt;/strong&gt;: How do thermal throttling thresholds differ between INT4 weight-only quantization and INT4 activation quantization during multi-turn generation?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


3. The 6-Stage Adversarial Gap Falsification Workflow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most researchers know the dread of reading 30 papers, designing a novel experiment, and discovering two weeks later that a lab in Zurich published that exact experiment six months ago.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In PaperTrail, candidate research gaps are never taken at face value. They are subjected to a rigorous &lt;strong&gt;6-Stage Adversarial Red-Team Audit&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[Candidate Gap Discovery]
│
▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Stage 1: Hypothesis Decomposition             │
│   Extract testable variables &amp;amp; parameters    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Stage 2: Targeted Counter-Evidence Search    │
│   Generate adversarial retrieval queries     │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Stage 3: Multi-Index Retrieval               │
│   Query OpenAlex, Crossref, arXiv, TinyFish  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Stage 4: Semantic Relevance &amp;amp; Overlap Audit  │
│   Compare baseline methodology &amp;amp; datasets    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Stage 5: Adversarial Refutation Scoring      │
│   Compute falsification confidence (0.0-1.0) │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Stage 6: Status Assignment                   │
│   SURVIVED (Validated) | FALSIFIED | PARTIAL │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Only when a candidate gap survives this adversarial gauntlet is it promoted to an actionable research gap. From there, PaperTrail automatically drafts an &lt;strong&gt;empirical experiment protocol&lt;/strong&gt; complete with hardware requirements, dataset references, compute hours, and verification checklists.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


4. Local-First Research Portability: SQLite + Supabase
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We believe your research belongs to you—not locked inside our cloud database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PaperTrail implements a dual-repository architecture:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight dot"&gt;&lt;code&gt;                  &lt;span class="err"&gt;┌───────────────────────────────┐&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="nv"&gt;ResearchRepository&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="err"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;Interface&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;└───────────────┬───────────────┘&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;┌────────────────┴────────────────┐&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;▼&lt;/span&gt;                                 &lt;span class="err"&gt;▼&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;┌───────────────────────────┐&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="err"&gt;┌───────────────────────────┐&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;SQLiteResearchRepository&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;SupabaseResearchRepository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="err"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;Node&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;native&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;DatabaseSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;)│&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="err"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;Cloud&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="err"&gt;│&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;└───────────────────────────┘&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="err"&gt;└───────────────────────────┘&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Using Node.js 22+'s native &lt;code&gt;DatabaseSync&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;node:sqlite&lt;/code&gt;), PaperTrail operates entirely offline if desired, writing directly to &lt;code&gt;.papertrail-data/papertrail.db&lt;/code&gt; with WAL mode enabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Complete Research Archive Export
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every research workspace can be exported with one click into a structured, vendor-neutral research bundle:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;papertrail-research/
├── manifest.json         # Cryptographic checksums, schema v1.0, timestamp
├── README.md             # Complete human-readable investigation brief
├── papers/               # Verified metadata, OpenAlex/arXiv DOIs, abstracts
├── claims/               # Grounded assertions with verbatim supporting quotes
├── evidence/             # Disagreement matrices and apparent vs genuine verdicts
├── graph/                # Interactive topology nodes and edges (JSON)
├── research-gaps/        # 6-stage falsification audits and counter-evidence
├── experiments/          # Resource-evaluated execution protocols
├── datasets/             # Hugging Face &amp;amp; Zenodo references with license terms
├── timeline/             # Evidence evolution and tracking history
└── provenance/           # Query origins and retrieval timestamps
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You can export this as a &lt;strong&gt;ZIP archive&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;BibTeX&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;JSON&lt;/strong&gt;, or formatted &lt;strong&gt;Markdown&lt;/strong&gt;, and import it into any other PaperTrail instance with zero data loss.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


5. Designing for Academics: The Academic Journal Aesthetic
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We made a conscious product design decision: &lt;strong&gt;PaperTrail should not look like another dark-mode SaaS dashboard with glowing neon gradients.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Science is deliberate, rigorous, and calm. We crafted a dedicated design language inspired by physical academic journals and high-end typography:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Warm Paper Surface&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;#F7F6F2&lt;/code&gt; (background), &lt;code&gt;#FDFCF9&lt;/code&gt; (cards), &lt;code&gt;#FAF8F3&lt;/code&gt; (alt panels)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deep Editorial Ink&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;#252622&lt;/code&gt; (primary text), &lt;code&gt;#777873&lt;/code&gt; (muted metadata)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Terracotta Accent&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;#B96645&lt;/code&gt; (reserved strictly for active claims, falsification audits, and disagreements)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sage Data Accent&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;#858B70&lt;/code&gt; (used for verified benchmark metrics, datasets, and lineages)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Typography&lt;/strong&gt;:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Instrument Serif&lt;/code&gt; for research questions, paper titles, and editorial headers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;DM Sans&lt;/code&gt; for high-density tabular data and claim cards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;JetBrains Mono&lt;/code&gt; for DOIs, citations, arXiv IDs, and hardware matrices.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;:root&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-bg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#F7F6F2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-surface&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#FDFCF9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-surface-alt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#FAF8F3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-ink&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#252622&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-muted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#777873&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-border&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#E3E1D9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-accent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#B96645&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--paper-data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#858B70&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The interface feels like reading a physical copy of &lt;em&gt;Nature&lt;/em&gt; or &lt;em&gt;Science&lt;/em&gt; paired with an interactive research workbench.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


6. Real-World Engineering Challenge: Taming Heavy LLMs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During our testing with local and remote Ollama models (specifically &lt;code&gt;gpt-oss:120b-cloud&lt;/code&gt;), we hit an interesting bottleneck.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When analyzing 8 papers simultaneously, each paper required claim extraction, experimental context parsing, pairwise contradiction checks, and gap discovery. Sending 15+ concurrent requests to a 120-billion parameter model over a cloud proxy resulted in request queueing that took 4+ minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We solved this with a three-tiered pipeline architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict 12s Timeout&lt;/strong&gt;: Ollama requests time out gracefully after 12 seconds instead of hanging the connection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Landmark Prioritization&lt;/strong&gt;: The top 3 landmark papers receive deep LLM extraction. Supporting literature receives instant, deterministic parsing (extracting findings, methodologies, and limitations in 0.1ms).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Capped Contradiction Audits&lt;/strong&gt;: Pairwise contradiction checking prioritizes the top 3 most divergent pairs rather than running an $O(N^2)$ cross-product.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This reduced the end-to-end pipeline execution time from &lt;strong&gt;4.5 minutes down to 14 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;, delivering a fast, responsive UX without sacrificing scientific rigor.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What’s Next?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PaperTrail is completely open source under the MIT license. Some of the features we're actively working on next:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TinyFish Continuous Web Monitoring&lt;/strong&gt;: Real-time tracking of lab publication feeds and author pages.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Automated Systematic Literature Review (SLR) Generator&lt;/strong&gt;: Exporting PRISMA-compliant flowcharts and evidence synthesis tables.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hardware-in-the-Loop Benchmark Validation&lt;/strong&gt;: Connecting experiment protocols directly to local MLX / llama.cpp runners for on-device replication.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're a researcher, engineer, or curious mind exploring literature:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⭐️ Star the repository on GitHub: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/umeshadabala/PaperTrail" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/umeshadabala/PaperTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check out the codebase, clone it, and run it locally with your own Ollama setup or cloud provider!&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let me know in the comments: &lt;em&gt;How do you currently track scientific contradictions and emerging research gaps in your field?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React segmented control is a radio group drawn as a row of 2 to 5 segments with a raised thumb on the selected one: one tab stop, arrow keys that move and select, and a 44px height on touch screens. Use it when the choice changes the content already on screen (a time range, a view, units); use Tabs when it swaps in a different panel, a Toggle Group when several options can be on, and radio cards when each option needs a description. The free Wingo UI Segmented Control adds the iOS details: a spring thumb you can drag sideways with a finger, while a vertical swipe still scrolls the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React segmented control is the row of two to five options with a raised thumb under the selected one, the control iOS uses to pick the time range of the charts in the Health app. Search for an iOS segmented control web component and most results are React Native packages, and shadcn/ui ships none as of October 2026. This guide covers what the web version has to get right (the thumb, touch, keyboard and radio semantics), then the decision that matters more: when Tabs, a Toggle Group or radio cards are the better control. The examples use the free &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Segmented Control&lt;/a&gt; from Wingo UI, the library we build, so we are not neutral; outside facts link to their sources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What is a segmented control, in web terms?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a radio group with a different look. Each segment is one option, exactly one is selected, and selecting one applies at once without a submit button. Apple's &lt;a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/segmented-controls" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Human Interface Guidelines&lt;/a&gt; describe it as a way to offer "closely related choices that affect an object, state, or view." Material Design 3 has a close relative, the &lt;a href="https://m3.material.io/components/segmented-buttons/overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;segmented button&lt;/a&gt;, so you also see the control searched as a React segmented button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That framing settles the implementation: the markup should be a &lt;code&gt;radiogroup&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;radio&lt;/code&gt; items, the &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/radio/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WAI-ARIA radio group pattern&lt;/a&gt; defines the keyboard, and a &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; should make it submit like native radios. The thumb, the spring and the drag sit on top of that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you add a segmented control in React?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install it, pass &lt;code&gt;options&lt;/code&gt;, and either let it hold its own value or control it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add segmented-control
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControlOption&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/segmented-control&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;periods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControlOption&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;week&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Week&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Month&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;SalesPeriod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPeriod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;week&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SegmentedControl&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Period"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;periods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onValueChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPeriod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fullWidth&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each option takes &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;label&lt;/code&gt;, and optionally &lt;code&gt;icon&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;badge&lt;/code&gt; (a number renders a count that hides at 0), &lt;code&gt;tooltip&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;disabled&lt;/code&gt;. The props that change the look are &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;sm&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;md&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;lg&lt;/code&gt;, a 32, 40 or 48px track), &lt;code&gt;radius&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;tone&lt;/code&gt; (default &lt;code&gt;neutral&lt;/code&gt;, a white thumb like iOS that turns a lifted gray in dark mode) or any CSS &lt;code&gt;color&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;dividers&lt;/code&gt;, the hairlines between unselected segments. Leave &lt;code&gt;defaultValue&lt;/code&gt; out and it starts on the first enabled option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Segmented Control.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (Tap or click Month, then press the thumb and slide it sideways: the value follows your finger and the sales total updates. With the keyboard, Tab in and use the arrow keys). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Segmented Control docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add segmented-control&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you came for a React segmented control Tailwind recipe, the whole look is Tailwind v4 utilities on design tokens, and every inner part takes classes through &lt;code&gt;classNames&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;item&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;thumb&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;icon&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;label&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;badge&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;divider&lt;/code&gt;). App-wide defaults go through the free &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/ui-config" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;UIProvider&lt;/a&gt;: &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;UIProvider defaults={{ "segmented-control": { radius: "full" } }}&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; rounds every segmented control in the app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What makes it feel like the iOS control?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three details: the thumb moves on a spring instead of a linear tween, it follows the finger, and it never steals a scroll.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The thumb animates transform only.&lt;/strong&gt; It is rendered inside the selected segment, so CSS sizes it at rest. When the value changes, it measures the box it is leaving, jumps there as a transform and springs back to zero offset using the &lt;code&gt;snappy&lt;/code&gt; spring from &lt;code&gt;lib/motion&lt;/code&gt; (stiffness 500, damping 32). Animating &lt;code&gt;left&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;width&lt;/code&gt; would recalculate layout on every frame; a transform stays on the compositor. Its corner radius is divided by the current scale, so a thumb stretching between a short and a long label keeps round corners. A change mid-flight starts from where the thumb is on screen and keeps its speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It answers the finger.&lt;/strong&gt; Pressing the thumb dips it to 96%. Keep holding and slide sideways and the value follows the segment under your finger, then settles where you let go. The hairlines next to the thumb fade out, like on iOS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A vertical swipe still scrolls the page.&lt;/strong&gt; The track sets &lt;code&gt;touch-action: pan-y&lt;/code&gt;, so the browser keeps vertical scrolling, and the code waits for 8px of movement before deciding what the gesture is. This is the part to copy if you build your own, trimmed from the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// track: className="touch-pan-y"; drag.current is recorded on pointerdown over the thumb, SLOP is 8&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;handlePointerMove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PointerEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;HTMLDivElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;drag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pointerId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientX&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vertical first: a page scroll, let the browser have it&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SLOP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;endPress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SLOP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentTarget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setPointerCapture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pointerId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// pick the segment under clientX from rects measured on pointerdown&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;touch-action: none&lt;/code&gt; on the track would make the drag simpler and trap anyone who starts a scroll on the control. After a drag, the click that follows lands on the segment where the drag began, so the component swallows it; otherwise the value snaps back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With reduced motion turned on, the thumb jumps to its new place and the press dip is off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Is a segmented control accessible?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is when the semantics are a radio group, and that comes almost free from Radix. The Segmented Control is built on &lt;code&gt;@radix-ui/react-radio-group&lt;/code&gt;, so the track is a &lt;code&gt;radiogroup&lt;/code&gt;, each segment a &lt;code&gt;radio&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;aria-checked&lt;/code&gt;, and the whole control is a single tab stop. Left and Right (Up and Down too) move focus and select, which matches the APG rule for a radio group outside a toolbar. We added Home and End, disabled segments are skipped, and &lt;code&gt;loop={false}&lt;/code&gt; stops the arrows at the ends. Mantine takes the same route: its &lt;a href="https://mantine.dev/core/segmented-control/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SegmentedControl docs&lt;/a&gt; (v9.7.1, October 2026) say it "uses radio inputs under the hood."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four things are on you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A name.&lt;/strong&gt; Pass &lt;code&gt;aria-label&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;aria-labelledby&lt;/code&gt; pointing at a visible heading; both land on the radiogroup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Text for icons.&lt;/strong&gt; Give icon options a &lt;code&gt;label&lt;/code&gt; anyway. With &lt;code&gt;iconOnly&lt;/code&gt;, it becomes the segment's &lt;code&gt;aria-label&lt;/code&gt; and its tooltip, shown on hover, on keyboard focus and on a long press on touch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A form name.&lt;/strong&gt; Pass &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; and, inside a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;form&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, the selected value submits like native radios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A non-drag path.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/dragging-movements.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WCAG 2.5.7 Dragging Movements&lt;/a&gt; (level AA) asks that anything done by dragging also work with a single pointer without dragging. Tapping a segment is that path, so keep it. Never make the drag the only way.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One trap if you build on Radix yourself: Radix checks the next radio only if the arrow key is still down when its deferred focus lands, so a very quick press (a screen reader, a remote, automation) can move focus without checking anything. The component selects on any focus that follows an arrow key, so a fast press still changes the value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should a segmented control behave on a phone?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should be 44px tall on touch, share the width, and never flash gray on tap. The &lt;code&gt;md&lt;/code&gt; size is a 40px track with a mouse and grows to 44px on coarse pointers (&lt;code&gt;pointer-coarse:h-11&lt;/code&gt;). The &lt;code&gt;sm&lt;/code&gt; size keeps its 32px look and gets an invisible hit area that reaches 44px; &lt;code&gt;lg&lt;/code&gt; is 48px everywhere. The reasoning behind 44px, and the Tailwind variants that apply it only on touch, are in &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/minimum-touch-target-size-react-tailwind" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;minimum touch target size for React and Tailwind&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;fullWidth&lt;/code&gt; gives each segment an equal share of the container, the usual layout under a phone header. A segment with a long label keeps its natural width instead of truncating, and the others share what is left. Every segment carries &lt;code&gt;touch-manipulation&lt;/code&gt; and a transparent &lt;code&gt;-webkit-tap-highlight-color&lt;/code&gt;, so a quick second tap never zooms the page and iOS shows no gray flash. Set &lt;code&gt;dir="rtl"&lt;/code&gt; and the layout, the arrow keys and the drag mirror.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rest of the rules (sheets instead of popovers, safe areas, hover alternatives) are in the pillar on &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/mobile-first-react-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mobile-first React components&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/category/mobile-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Mobile UI in React&lt;/a&gt; category collects every guide on the topic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Segmented control vs tabs, a toggle group or radio cards: which one?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick by what the choice changes. The look tells you little, since Wingo UI's Tabs have a &lt;code&gt;segmented&lt;/code&gt; variant with the same raised thumb. What differs is the semantics, and with them what a screen reader user expects to happen next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;The choice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What assistive tech gets&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Wingo UI&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Changes the content already on screen: a time range, a view, units; 2 to 5 short options&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Segmented Control&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;radiogroup&lt;/code&gt; of &lt;code&gt;radio&lt;/code&gt;; arrows move and select&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Swaps in a different panel of content&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/tabs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tabs&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;code&gt;variant="segmented"&lt;/code&gt; for this look&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;tablist&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;tab&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;tabpanel&lt;/code&gt; linked by &lt;code&gt;aria-controls&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Changes the URL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/nav-tabs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nav Tabs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;links in a &lt;code&gt;nav&lt;/code&gt;, the current one with &lt;code&gt;aria-current="page"&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pro&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Several options can be on at once (bold and italic, amenities)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/toggle-group" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Toggle Group&lt;/a&gt; &lt;code&gt;type="multiple"&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;buttons with &lt;code&gt;aria-pressed&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pro&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Each option needs a description or a price&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/radio-group" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Radio Group&lt;/a&gt; &lt;code&gt;variant="card"&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;radiogroup&lt;/code&gt; of &lt;code&gt;radio&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More than five options, or long labels&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/select" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Select&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a &lt;code&gt;combobox&lt;/code&gt; that opens a &lt;code&gt;listbox&lt;/code&gt; (a bottom sheet on phones)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Apple draws a similar line on iOS: "For switching between completely separate sections of an app, use a tab bar instead." Here are the same segments as Tabs, each with its own panel:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Tabs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/tabs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ProfileForm and BillingForm are your own components&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;AccountTabs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tabs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"segmented"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fullWidth&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Account"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProfileForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;billing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Billing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BillingForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Tabs.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (The same thumb as the segmented control, but each segment opens its own panel: switch from Active to Sold and the list slides in from the side you moved toward). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/tabs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tabs docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add tabs&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Toggle Group needs one more note. In single mode Radix also renders it as a &lt;code&gt;radiogroup&lt;/code&gt;, but its arrow keys only move focus and Space or Enter presses the item. That is the APG rule for a radio group inside a toolbar, where "the button that is checked does not change" while you arrow through. So an alignment switch in an editor toolbar is a single Toggle Group, and a stand-alone time range is a segmented control. When several can be on, the Toggle Group in &lt;code&gt;multiple&lt;/code&gt; mode is the only one of the four that fits:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ToggleGroup&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/toggle-group&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;AmenityFilter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ToggleGroup&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"multiple"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"outline"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Amenities"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;parking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;parking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Parking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;pets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Pets allowed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;laundry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;In-unit laundry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Toggle Group.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (Bold starts on. Press Italic and both stay on, because the formatting group is type multiple; the alignment group next to it is single, so pressing one releases the other). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/toggle-group" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Toggle Group docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add toggle-group&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Radio cards win when the options are not self-explanatory. "Courier, Locker, Pickup" fits in a segmented control; "Home delivery, FedEx, 1-2 days, $9.99" does not, and squeezing it into a segment hides the price that decides the choice. The &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-form-components-guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React form components guide&lt;/a&gt; covers the wider choice between a radio group, a select and a combobox in a form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What goes wrong with segmented controls?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four things: too many segments, icons mixed with text, a value outside the options, and a missing name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Too many segments.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple says "no more than about five segments on iPhone", and &lt;a href="https://api.flutter.dev/flutter/material/SegmentedButton-class.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Flutter's SegmentedButton docs&lt;/a&gt; say they are "typically used in cases where there are only 2-5 options." Six or more labels on a 360px screen get cramped or cut off; switch to a select or scrolling tabs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mixing icons and text.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple's guidelines prefer one or the other in a single control. Pick text, or &lt;code&gt;iconOnly&lt;/code&gt; with labels as names.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A controlled value that is not in &lt;code&gt;options&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; The control shows no thumb, which looks broken. This usually happens when options load after the value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No name.&lt;/strong&gt; A radiogroup without &lt;code&gt;aria-label&lt;/code&gt; reads as an unnamed group of radios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does a complete example look like?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An orders screen that filters one list by status. The choice changes the content on screen and there are four short options, so this is the segmented control's job. Counts ride on badges, and a status line tells screen readers how many orders are visible:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControlOption&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/segmented-control&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;shipped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;returned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;usd&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Intl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NumberFormat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en-US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;currency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;currency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;USD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;OrdersPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SegmentedControlOption&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;All&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;badge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;shipped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Shipped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;returned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Returned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;badge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;returned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex flex-col gap-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SegmentedControl&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Order status"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onValueChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fullWidth&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"sm:max-w-md"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-sm text-muted-foreground"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex flex-col divide-y divide-border"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex justify-between gap-4 py-3 text-sm"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"truncate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"tabular-nums"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;usd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In a form, drop the state and give it a &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt;. A server action then reads it like any radio:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;saveDelivery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SegmentedControl&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"delivery"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Delivery"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"locker"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fullWidth&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;courier&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Courier&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;locker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Locker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;pickup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Pickup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* in saveDelivery: formData.get("delivery") */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Where should you start?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install the free control and drop it where a time range, a view switch or a unit toggle lives today:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add segmented-control
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Tabs and Radio Group are free too. The Toggle Group and Nav Tabs from the table above are part of &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Wingo UI Pro&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Components in this post
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Segmented Control&lt;/a&gt; (Free): The iOS segmented control: pick one of a few options on a raised thumb that slides, and on touch drags along with your finger. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add segmented-control&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/tabs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tabs&lt;/a&gt; (Free): Switch between views of the same page with a sliding indicator: underline for sections, pill for filters, segmented for a compact switch. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add tabs&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/toggle-group" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Toggle Group&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): A row of toggles where one or several stay pressed, for text formatting, alignment, view switches and quick filters. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add toggle-group&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/radio-group" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Radio Group&lt;/a&gt; (Free): Pick exactly one of a few visible options: rows with a dot that springs in, or cards with icons, prices and a selection ring that slides. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add radio-group&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does shadcn/ui have a segmented control?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not as of October 2026. Its component list has Tabs, Toggle Group, Radio Group and Button Group, so building one on shadcn/ui means restyling one of those. Pick the base by what the choice does: Tabs for panels, a radio group for a value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is the difference between a segmented control and tabs?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tabs swap in a different panel and expose tablist, tab and tabpanel roles linked by aria-controls. A segmented control picks a value that changes the content already on screen, such as a time range, and is a radiogroup. Apple's guidelines send switching between separate sections of an app to a tab bar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How many options should a segmented control have?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two to five. Apple's Human Interface Guidelines say no more than about five segments on iPhone, and Flutter's Material SegmentedButton docs say segmented buttons are typically used with 2 to 5 options. Past five, use a select or a scrolling row of tabs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is a segmented control accessible to screen readers?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is when it is built as a radiogroup of radio items with a name: one tab stop, arrow keys that move and check, and aria-checked on the selected segment. Icon-only segments also need a text name, which Wingo UI's iconOnly mode takes from each option's label.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can a segmented control submit with a form?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Give Wingo UI's Segmented Control a name and, inside a form, it submits the selected value like a radio group, so FormData and server actions read it with formData.get.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is the Wingo UI Segmented Control free?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. The Segmented Control, Tabs, Radio Group and Select are free and install with npx wingo-ui@latest add. The Toggle Group and Nav Tabs are part of Wingo UI Pro.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on the Wingo UI blog: &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-segmented-control" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Segmented Control: iOS Thumb, Drag and When to Use It&lt;/a&gt;. The original has the live, interactive demos and is kept up to date.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As developers, we've all been there: you spend 3 to 6 months building something useful, you submit it to traditional launch platforms, and within three hours you're buried under VC-funded tools, sponsored upvotes, and timezone cutoffs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of just complaining about it, I decided to engineer a technical solution: &lt;strong&gt;Astro Discover&lt;/strong&gt; — a clean product discovery engine and launchpad with a synchronized 24-hour spotlight window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is an honest engineering retrospective of how I architected this using &lt;strong&gt;React, Vite, Firebase Firestore, and Google AI Studio&lt;/strong&gt;, including the hard constraints and edge cases we solved.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


1. System Requirements &amp;amp; Core Constraints
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before writing code, I set three non-negotiable architectural requirements:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deterministic 24-Hour Windows:&lt;/strong&gt; Products must not launch at arbitrary hours. Every scheduled product must go live at exactly 12:01 AM IST and stay on the front page for 24 continuous hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fail-Safe Queue Processing:&lt;/strong&gt; If a manual security review is pending when the scheduled launch date hits, the system must not delay the launch. It must automatically display the project with a "Pending Review" badge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sub-Second TTFB &amp;amp; Direct Hydration:&lt;/strong&gt; No flashy loading overlays, no bloated client-side trackers. Immediate first contentful paint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


2. The Tech Stack &amp;amp; Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Frontend: React + TypeScript + Vite
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-Flicker Routing:&lt;/strong&gt; Core pages (Home, Today, Explore) are bundled directly for instant initial paint, while secondary management routes (Admin, Dashboard, Bug Reporting) are code-split using dynamic imports.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS Utility Styling:&lt;/strong&gt; Clean dark/light theme tokens without external component framework bloat.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deterministic Date Utilities:&lt;/strong&gt; We wrote custom normalization helpers (&lt;code&gt;parseFirestoreDate&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;isScheduledOrCreatedTodayIST&lt;/code&gt;) to ensure browser timezone offsets never cause hydration mismatches or &lt;code&gt;Invalid Date&lt;/code&gt; crashes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Backend: Real-time Reactive Firestore
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Firestore provides real-time snapshot listeners for upvotes and submissions without having to maintain a dedicated WebSocket fleet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Firestore Security Rules enforce strict role-based access control (RBAC), ensuring makers can only modify their own submissions and upvotes cannot be artificially spoofed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


3. Engineering Challenges We Solved
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Timezone Normalization Problem
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When makers schedule from San Francisco (PST) vs London (GMT) vs Tokyo (JST), raw JavaScript &lt;code&gt;new Date()&lt;/code&gt; calculations can split a launch across two calendar dates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We solved this by anchoring our launch engine strictly to &lt;strong&gt;Indian Standard Time (IST, UTC+5:30)&lt;/strong&gt; at the data layer. Dates are stored as canonical &lt;code&gt;YYYY-MM-DD&lt;/code&gt; strings alongside epoch timestamps, guaranteeing that every user across the globe sees the exact same daily launch batch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Eliminating Client-Side "Loading Jitter"
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During early prototyping, we had temporary Suspense fallback spinners showing "Loading..." screens. For a developer audience, that feels slow and unpolished. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We reworked the component tree:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Empty states and skeleton placeholders are kept minimal and inline.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Root HTML is completely clean, allowing the React tree to hydrate directly with zero flashing text or spinners.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


4. Key Takeaways for Solo Builders Using AI Studio
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a production-ready application with Google AI Studio taught me a few crucial lessons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Design the schema first:&lt;/strong&gt; AI performs best when you give it clear interfaces (e.g., explicit TypeScript types for &lt;code&gt;Project&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;LaunchStatus&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;StartupStage&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Own your edge cases:&lt;/strong&gt; AI will scaffold standard CRUD operations effortlessly, but timezone math, security rules, and queue fallbacks require human oversight and precise constraints.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Focus on developer utility:&lt;/strong&gt; Features like 1-click tech stack inspection, GitHub repo links, and license filtering resonated far more with users than fancy animations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Try Astro Discover
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The platform is open, active, and live right now:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;strong&gt;Explore the Directory:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://astrodiscover.ai.studio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://astrodiscover.ai.studio&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
👉 &lt;strong&gt;Submit Your Side Project:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://astrodiscover.ai.studio/submit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://astrodiscover.ai.studio/submit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’d love to hear your feedback on the architecture, directory performance, or submission workflow. If you have an indie tool or open-source repo, drop your link below — I’d love to check it out!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a lot of remote meetings, and the first five minutes are usually silence and black camera tiles. So I built a set of small multiplayer games that run in the browser: Draw &amp;amp; Guess, Two Truths and a Lie, Emoji Charades, Zoom In and a few more. Everyone joins from one link, with no accounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was simple. The bugs were more interesting, so here are three of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. The game kept showing the answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zoom In shows a mystery picture as an extreme close-up, then slowly zooms out. Whoever names it first scores the most.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Playing it, I noticed that each round flashed the whole picture for a moment before zooming in. That ruins the game.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cause was React doing its job. The picture element was reused from one round to the next. At the end of a round it was fully zoomed out, and a 0.25-second CSS transition animated it back up to full zoom when the next picture arrived. For a quarter of a second, you could see the new picture at its full size.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is one prop. Key the element by puzzle, so each picture mounts fresh instead of animating from the old state:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"zi-view"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-hidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If a CSS transition ever shows you something it shouldn't between two states, ask whether those should be the same element at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Nobody owned the clock
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A party game has to keep moving on its own, and the obvious design has a flaw: if only the host's browser moves the game on and the host's Wi-Fi drops, the round just sits there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The game state is a pure reducer over an action log. Time comes from &lt;code&gt;action.at&lt;/code&gt; and randomness from &lt;code&gt;action.nonce&lt;/code&gt;, so every client that replays the same log reaches the same state. That means any client can safely push the game forward. The problem becomes who should do it, and when.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The answer is a staggered timer. The host and the current drawer fire on time, and everyone else waits a few seconds:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;driver&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isDrawer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;me&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;room&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ownerId&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;late&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;driver&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3500&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fire&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;at&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;late&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fired&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fired&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;act&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Normally the host or drawer fires first. If they vanish, everyone else steps in 3.5 seconds later. The room may send the same action more than once, so each action carries a &lt;code&gt;turnId&lt;/code&gt; and the reducer ignores any that no longer apply.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. My analytics had been wrong from day one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one isn't about games, but it cost me more than the others.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrote a tiny cookie-free page counter, with the endpoint in a constant:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;https://…&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Further down, the code called &lt;code&gt;new URL(document.referrer)&lt;/code&gt; to read where a visitor came from. That line was now trying to construct a string. It threw, a &lt;code&gt;catch&lt;/code&gt; returned an empty value, and every visit was recorded with no referrer. 217 page views later I noticed that not one of them had a source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I spent two days making decisions about traffic with no idea where it came from. Renaming the constant took ten seconds. The lesson I took is to test your measurement with a known input on the day you ship it, because a counter that goes up looks like a counter that works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it, or tell me what's broken
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The games are free with no sign-up, and they run on React, Vite, Vercel and Supabase realtime channels: &lt;a href="https://fun-retro.com/games/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_campaign=three-bugs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;fun-retro.com/games&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd especially like to hear about lag or sync glitches with five or more people in a room. How do you handle timers when any client can disappear?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I build these with AI as a pair-programming partner, and this post was drafted with AI assistance from the real code and bug history.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Aether AI Studio
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A local-first AI workstation: chat, an autonomous coding agent, and image/video generation in one interface.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most AI front-ends either lock you into one provider or send everything to the cloud. &lt;strong&gt;Aether AI Studio&lt;/strong&gt; lets you decide. Run models locally with Ollama or LM Studio and nothing leaves your machine. When you need more power, switch to OpenAI, Anthropic, or Gemini from the same UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Latest release (Windows launcher): &lt;a href="https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio/releases/latest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio/releases/latest&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Current version: &lt;strong&gt;v4.1.1&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


💬 Multi-provider chat
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One &lt;code&gt;UnifiedLLMClient&lt;/code&gt; fronts Ollama, LM Studio, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Gemini, with SSE streaming.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Personas with their own system prompts and per-persona tool access.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rich Markdown rendering: code highlighting, LaTeX math, tables, and nested inline formatting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hardware-aware settings:&lt;/strong&gt; when you raise max tokens or the context window, the app estimates whether your RAM/GPU can handle it and warns you before you hit an out-of-memory wall.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


🤖 Code mode: a real autonomous agent
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is not a "paste code, get code" box. The agent plans, reads files, edits, runs commands, and verifies its own work in a loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;15 built-in tools&lt;/strong&gt; (each can be toggled individually in Settings):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_dir&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;glob&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;grep&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;read_file&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;edit_file&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;multi_edit&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;write_file&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;run_command&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;git&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;check_syntax&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;web_fetch&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;web_search&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;todo_write&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;delete_path&lt;/code&gt; · &lt;code&gt;move_path&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Things I cared about getting right:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Approval gate:&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;em&gt;Accept Edit&lt;/em&gt; mode every change is shown as a diff and waits for your OK.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Anti-cheating guards:&lt;/strong&gt; the agent cannot "fix" failing tests by deleting existing functions or rewriting test assertions unless explicitly allowed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Failure circuit breaker:&lt;/strong&gt; repeated failing tool calls trigger a warning, then a hard stop, so it can't loop forever.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context budgeting:&lt;/strong&gt; long sessions are trimmed automatically to fit the model's window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Resumable sessions:&lt;/strong&gt; transcripts are persisted, so you can pick up where you left off.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Works with weak models too:&lt;/strong&gt; if a model has no native tool-calling, the agent falls back to parsing tool calls from text.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


🎨 Media Studio
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Images:&lt;/strong&gt; Pollinations (free, no key), OpenAI &lt;code&gt;gpt-image-1&lt;/code&gt;, Gemini Imagen, Stable Diffusion WebUI.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Video:&lt;/strong&gt; OpenAI Sora and Gemini Veo (API key required).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chat personas can call &lt;code&gt;generate_image&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;generate_video&lt;/code&gt; directly, and results render inline in the conversation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


✨ AI Prompt Enhancer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turn a rough idea into a structured prompt, using either an LLM or a template. It's multilingual and answers in the language you choose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


🌐 Internationalization
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traditional Chinese, Simplified Chinese, English, and Japanese. That's 650+ UI strings, plus the README in all four languages. The top bar measures available width and collapses buttons level by level, so it doesn't break when you switch languages or close the sidebar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Layer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tech&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python, FastAPI, SQLite, SSE&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Frontend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React 18, Vite, Tailwind CSS, TypeScript&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;LLM layer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;UnifiedLLMClient&lt;/code&gt; over 5 providers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Desktop / launcher&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tauri (Rust) launcher, Electron shell&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Extras&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Telegram bridge, optional PIN-protected web access&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A small detail I'm happy with: the version number lives only in the root &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt;. Vite injects it into the sidebar badge and window title at build time, and a test fails if the backend, frontend, desktop app, or launcher drift out of sync. (I learned that one the hard way when a release shipped with a stale badge.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Quick start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Download &lt;code&gt;AetherLauncher.exe&lt;/code&gt; from the &lt;a href="https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio/releases/latest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;latest release&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run it; it starts the backend and frontend for you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In Settings, pick a model source: local Ollama / LM Studio, or paste a cloud API key.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chat, or switch to &lt;strong&gt;Code mode&lt;/strong&gt;, point it at a project folder, and let the agent work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prefer running from source? The repo has the FastAPI backend and Vite frontend with setup steps in the README.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who is it for?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;People who want to run models locally and keep their data private&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developers who want a Cursor / Claude Code-style agent experience with full control over every edit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creators who want text, image, and video generation in one tool&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Feedback welcome
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Video generation is the least-tested part (it needs paid API keys), so bug reports there are especially appreciated. Stars, issues, and PRs are all welcome:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/hankyleisplay/AetherAI-Studio&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The map told you where each piece goes. This one follows a single run, moment by moment, and shows the code that renders each one.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Two days ago we drew &lt;a href="https://medium.com/@cuelarkofficial/where-everything-goes-in-an-ai-agent-interface-fde7972eb4f1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the map of an agent interface&lt;/a&gt;: seven places every agent app shares, and which component belongs in each. That answered &lt;em&gt;where&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post answers &lt;em&gt;when&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;how&lt;/em&gt;. An agent run is a sequence of moments: someone &lt;strong&gt;asks&lt;/strong&gt;, the agent &lt;strong&gt;thinks&lt;/strong&gt;, it &lt;strong&gt;acts&lt;/strong&gt;, it stops to get an &lt;strong&gt;approval&lt;/strong&gt;, it &lt;strong&gt;answers&lt;/strong&gt;, and someone who cares about cost or bugs &lt;strong&gt;observes&lt;/strong&gt; the run afterwards. Cuelark UI's agent kit is 29 React components for shadcn/ui, grouped by exactly those moments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We'll follow one run through all six, with the real props for every component. The run is fictional (file names, counts and timings are made up for the example), but every API is the one in the registry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The run:&lt;/strong&gt; "Invoice totals are a cent off for EU customers. Find out why and fix it."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One line in &lt;code&gt;components.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"registries"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"@cuelark"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://ui.cuelark.com/r/{name}.json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then install by name, for example &lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add @cuelark/reasoning @cuelark/tool @cuelark/plan&lt;/code&gt;. Each component lands in &lt;code&gt;components/ui/&lt;/code&gt; as source you own. The &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/docs/installation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;installation guide&lt;/a&gt; covers the by-URL route and the shadcn MCP server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Ask
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prompt Input · Queue · Model Selector&lt;/strong&gt; (and Suggestions)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PromptInput&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;files&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onStop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;toolbar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ModelSelector&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onValueChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;status="streaming"&lt;/code&gt; is the one prop that matters most: it swaps Send for Stop and binds Esc. The &lt;code&gt;toolbar&lt;/code&gt; slot is where Model Selector lives; it's a compact pill that opens a searchable list grouped by provider, with each model's context window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thirty seconds in, the user remembers credit notes. The agent is busy, so the follow-up waits:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Queue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;queued&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onItemsChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQueued&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Alt+↑/↓ reorders, × removes, and the agent takes items one at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt; if your agent answers in two seconds, skip Queue. If you only have one model, skip Model Selector. Prompt Input is never optional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Think
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reasoning · Chain of Thought · Task&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model starts thinking. Stream it, then let it get out of the way:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Reasoning&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isThinking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reasoningText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When &lt;code&gt;streaming&lt;/code&gt; flips to false, it folds to "Thought for 6s". Omit &lt;code&gt;duration&lt;/code&gt; and it measures itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once there's an approach, named steps read better than raw thought:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThought&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Tracking down the rounding error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThoughtStep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Searched for tax calculation"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThoughtStep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Read src/tax.ts and src/invoice.ts"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThoughtStep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"active"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Checking EU VAT rounding rules"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThoughtStep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"pending"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Propose a fix and run tests"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThought&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Steps default to &lt;code&gt;"complete"&lt;/code&gt;, so you only mark the active and pending ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt; three components, three shapes of thinking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reasoning&lt;/strong&gt; for free text the model produced. You don't control its structure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chain of Thought&lt;/strong&gt; for steps your orchestrator names, where order matters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Task&lt;/strong&gt; for a checklist with a progress bar, where order doesn't: &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Task title="Migrate invoices" items={[{ label: "Write helper", status: "done" }]} /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake is rendering orchestrator steps inside Reasoning. Then the one thing the user wants to scan is in the faded window they've learned to ignore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Act
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tool · Subagent · Terminal · File Diff · Test Results&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent searches the code and fetches the VAT guidance:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"search_code"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"success"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;roundTax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"web_fetch"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"running"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vatGuideUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; runs &lt;code&gt;"pending"&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;"running"&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;"success"&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;"error"&lt;/code&gt;. Keep &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; as the real tool name, not a friendly label. People learn &lt;code&gt;web_fetch&lt;/code&gt; in one run, and the real name is what they'll paste into a bug report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Between calls there's sometimes a beat with nothing to inspect. That's not a Tool; it's a status line:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TextShimmer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Comparing rounding modes…&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TextShimmer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Text Shimmer is the kit's status line for "doing something right now": CSS-only, and plain muted text under reduced motion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the agent hands the tests to a helper:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Subagent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"test-runner"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Run the invoice suite"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"running"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;activity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Running vitest…"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Terminal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"vitest"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;testsRunning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Subagent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sub-agent's own transcript sits on an indented rail inside it. When it's finished, set &lt;code&gt;status="done"&lt;/code&gt; and pass &lt;code&gt;duration&lt;/code&gt;. Once the run completes, swap the console for structure:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TestResults&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;suites&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;invoice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;rounds EU totals once&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;pass&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;keeps US line rounding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;pass&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A suite with a failure opens itself, failing assertion under the test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the change itself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;FileDiff&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"src/tax.ts"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;export function roundTax(items: LineItem[]) {&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;remove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;  return items.map((i) =&amp;gt; round(i.tax)).reduce(sum, 0);&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;  return round(items.map((i) =&amp;gt; i.tax).reduce(sum, 0));&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onAccept&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onReject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;discard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Unchanged runs fold after six lines (&lt;code&gt;foldAfter&lt;/code&gt;), and the header records Accept or Reject.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Terminal&lt;/strong&gt; is the live process; &lt;strong&gt;Test Results&lt;/strong&gt; is the verdict. Show Terminal while it runs, Test Results when it's done. Most apps show both, stacked.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File Diff&lt;/strong&gt; is a decision; a plain Code Block is just code. If you want a yes or no, it's a diff.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Subagent&lt;/strong&gt; whenever a different agent did the work, even a short one. Nesting is the only thing that keeps "who did this?" answerable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Approve
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plan · Confirmation · Checkpoint&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before touching code, the agent proposes a plan:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Plan&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Round the invoice total once in roundTax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Call it after summing in invoice.ts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Run the invoice suite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Open a draft pull request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onApprove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onReject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;autoRun&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;autoRun={10}&lt;/code&gt; drains a ring on Run; the plan starts unless someone cancels. We use it for low-stakes plans in trusted workspaces and leave it off everywhere else. &lt;code&gt;visible&lt;/code&gt; (default 4) sets how many steps show before folding; &lt;code&gt;shortcuts={false}&lt;/code&gt; frees ⌘Enter and Esc if your app needs them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later the agent wants to rewrite stored totals on staging. One action, real side effects:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Confirmation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;risk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"high"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Recalculate 214 staging invoices"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Rewrites stored totals with the new rounding."&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"pnpm tsx scripts/recalc-invoices.ts --env=staging"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onApprove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onDeny&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;skip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;risk="high"&lt;/code&gt; turns approve into press-and-hold. The command is shown verbatim, so what you approve is what runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And before the diff is applied, a way back:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Checkpoint&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Before rounding fix"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"14:02"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onRestore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;restore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt; Plan approves &lt;em&gt;intent&lt;/em&gt; across steps. Confirmation approves &lt;em&gt;one action&lt;/em&gt;. Checkpoint isn't an approval at all; it's the undo you place right before anything you might regret. Approving a plan doesn't remove the need for a Checkpoint; they answer different questions. (For reloads, both Plan and Confirmation render past decisions as receipts. We covered that in the map post, so we won't repeat it here.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Streaming Text · Inline Citation · Sources · Artifact · Branch&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The answer streams:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StreamingText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;answering&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;New chunks fade up out of a slight blur; text already on screen never moves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The claim about VAT gets its evidence right next to it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
For this invoice type, VAT is rounded on the total, not per line
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;InlineCitation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;guideTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vatGuideUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;excerpt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;quote&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sources&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;sources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;guideTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vatGuideUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;src/tax.ts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fileUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the agent also wrote a summary for the finance team, that's too big for a message:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Artifact&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Document"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Rounding fix: summary"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClose&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onDownload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;download&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Artifact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the user regenerates the answer, keep the old one:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Branch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;versions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;firstAnswer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;secondAnswer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onIndexChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Inline Citation and Sources&lt;/strong&gt; are a pair, not alternatives. A Sources list on its own makes people guess which source backs which sentence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Artifact&lt;/strong&gt; for anything people will read full screen or download; the message just points at it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Branch&lt;/strong&gt; only when there really are versions. Don't show "1 / 1".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Observe
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Context Meter · Trace Waterfall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This moment isn't for every user. It's for whoever pays the bill or fixes the bug.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ContextMeter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;84000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;200000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;cost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.42&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;breakdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cached&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reasoning&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A ring that warms from ink to amber to red as the context fills, with the token breakdown and cost on hover. (Figures above are example values.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the whole run on one timeline:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TraceWaterfall&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;spans&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;fix-eu-rounding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;41&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;m1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1840&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;t1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;search_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;6.8&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;t2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;web_fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;9.5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;sa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;test-runner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;22&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;39&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;t3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;vitest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;23&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;38&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;kind&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;"agent"&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;"llm"&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;"tool"&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;start&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;end&lt;/code&gt; are seconds from the start of the run; leave &lt;code&gt;end&lt;/code&gt; off and the span grows live. Set &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt; on a span and it turns red. Hover any bar for duration, tokens and cost. You can see it with real interaction on the &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/components/trace-waterfall" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trace Waterfall page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which one when:&lt;/strong&gt; Chain of Thought is the user's view of progress, inside the turn. Trace Waterfall is the developer's view of the whole run, in the run header or a debug panel. If you put a waterfall in a consumer chat thread, you've built a dashboard nobody asked for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The whole turn, composed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything above, as one assistant turn. Data loading is yours; every prop is real.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;AgentTurn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"assistant"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MessageContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* think */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Reasoning&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;thinking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThought&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThoughtStep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChainOfThought&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* act */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* approve */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Plan&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onApprove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;approve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onReject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;defaultState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;planDecision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* act, delegated */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Subagent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"test-runner"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Run the invoice suite"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TestResults&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;suites&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;suites&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Subagent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* approve, then act */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Checkpoint&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Before rounding fix"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;checkpointAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onRestore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;restore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;FileDiff&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"src/tax.ts"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;diff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onAccept&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onReject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;discard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* answer */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StreamingText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;answering&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sources&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;sources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MessageContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Outside the thread, the observe moment:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex items-center justify-end gap-3"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ContextMeter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;cost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TraceWaterfall&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;spans&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;trace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Read the comments top to bottom and you get the run: think, act, approve, act, answer. The order of components &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; the story of the run. If your JSX reads in an order that doesn't match what happened, your users will be confused in the same place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this run taught us
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One moment, one component.&lt;/strong&gt; The kit has three ways to show thinking because there are three shapes of thinking. Pick by shape, not by looks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Defaults are compact, errors aren't.&lt;/strong&gt; A failed Tool and a failing test suite open themselves. Everything else waits for a click.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Approvals come in three kinds.&lt;/strong&gt; Intent (Plan), action (Confirmation), and the way back (Checkpoint). Most apps ship only the middle one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Observe lives outside the thread.&lt;/strong&gt; Context Meter and Trace Waterfall are for the people who pay for or debug the run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fastest way to learn the kit is to take one apart. On the &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;live agent demos&lt;/a&gt;, hover any part of the coding, research or data agent and it names itself; click the tag to open its page and copy the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cuelark UI is free and open source (MIT): &lt;a href="https://github.com/cuelark/ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/cuelark/ui&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What are AI agent UI components?&lt;/strong&gt; Components for the moments of an agent run: asking, thinking, acting, approving, answering and observing. A chat kit covers asking and answering; an agent kit covers the four in between.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How many agent components does Cuelark UI have?&lt;/strong&gt; 29. By moment: ask (Prompt Input, Queue, Model Selector, Suggestions), think (Reasoning, Chain of Thought, Task, Text Shimmer), act (Tool, Subagent, Terminal, File Diff, Test Results, Stack Trace, Code Block, File Tree, Web Preview), approve (Plan, Confirmation, Checkpoint), answer (Conversation, Message, Streaming Text, Inline Citation, Sources, Artifact, Branch) and observe (Context Meter, Trace Waterfall).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do they work with the Vercel AI SDK?&lt;/strong&gt; Yes, or with any stream. They take plain props such as &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;input&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;output&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;steps&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;spans&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Are they shadcn/ui components?&lt;/strong&gt; Yes. They install as source with the shadcn CLI and use your Tailwind CSS v4 theme tokens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting tokens from a model to the browser is the easy part. Every modern SDK does it in a few lines. The hard part is everything the user actually experiences while those tokens arrive: the view that yanks them back to the bottom while they are trying to read, the Send button that does nothing while a reply runs, the paragraph that reflows every 40 milliseconds, the screen reader that either announces nothing or announces every single token.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide builds a streaming chat interface in React that gets those details right. We will use shadcn/ui as the foundation, the Vercel AI SDK for the transport, and four open-source components from Cuelark UI for the parts shadcn does not ship: &lt;code&gt;Conversation&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Message&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;StreamingText&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;PromptInput&lt;/code&gt;. Every component is installed as source, so if you disagree with a decision we made, you can change the file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By the end you will have:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a scroll container that follows a streaming reply and stops following the moment the reader scrolls up;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a composer that turns Send into Stop, and lets Esc cancel a run;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;streamed text that never moves what is already on screen;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;sensible accessibility defaults for all of the above.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a streaming chat needs beyond a message list
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before writing code, it helps to name the problems. A static list of messages is a solved problem. Streaming adds four new ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scroll ownership.&lt;/strong&gt; While a reply streams, the view should stick to the bottom. The instant the reader scrolls up, the interface must stop chasing new tokens, because the reader has told you they want to read something else. When they want to come back, they need a fast way to do it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interruptibility.&lt;/strong&gt; A reply in progress is a running process. If the model has misunderstood the question, the user should be able to stop it immediately, from the keyboard as well as the mouse. A text box with no Stop button during streaming feels broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Visual stability.&lt;/strong&gt; Each chunk that arrives can cause the paragraph to reflow. Words that were already read should not move, flash or re-render with a different weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Announcements.&lt;/strong&gt; Assistive technology should hear that a reply is arriving without being flooded token by token.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The four components below map one to one onto those problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Set up the project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You need a React project configured for shadcn/ui with Tailwind CSS v4. Next.js, Vite, TanStack Start, React Router and Astro all work. If you are starting fresh:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then add the Cuelark registry to &lt;code&gt;components.json&lt;/code&gt; once, so you can install components by name:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"registries"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"@cuelark"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://ui.cuelark.com/r/{name}.json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now install the four chat components:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add @cuelark/conversation @cuelark/message @cuelark/streaming-text @cuelark/prompt-input
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If you would rather not touch &lt;code&gt;components.json&lt;/code&gt;, each component also installs by URL, for example &lt;code&gt;npx shadcn@latest add https://ui.cuelark.com/r/conversation.json&lt;/code&gt;. The CLI brings the theme tokens and the motion layer along with whatever you add. The full set of options is on the &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/docs/installation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;installation page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, install the AI SDK packages:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;ai @ai-sdk/react @ai-sdk/openai
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The examples use OpenAI, but any AI SDK provider works the same way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. The route handler
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server side is short. This is a Next.js App Router route; the shape is similar for other frameworks.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/api/chat/route.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@ai-sdk/openai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;streamText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;convertToModelMessages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;UIMessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;UIMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;streamText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;gpt-4o-mini&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;convertToModelMessages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toUIMessageStreamResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The snippets here are written against AI SDK 5's &lt;code&gt;useChat&lt;/code&gt; and UI message stream. If you are on a different major version, the hook names may differ slightly; the UI layer does not change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. The scroll container: Conversation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the skeleton of the chat page.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useChat&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@ai-sdk/react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Conversation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ConversationContent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/conversation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;MessageContent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StreamingText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/streaming-text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PromptInput&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/prompt-input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Chat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sendMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;stop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useChat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isStreaming&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;submitted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"mx-auto flex h-dvh max-w-3xl flex-col gap-4 p-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Conversation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex-1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConversationContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* messages go here */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConversationContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Conversation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PromptInput&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isStreaming&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onStop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Conversation&lt;/code&gt; is the piece that handles scroll ownership. It follows the newest message while a reply streams and stops following as soon as the reader scrolls up. When they are away from the bottom, a "Latest" pill appears to jump back. You do not wire any of this yourself: no &lt;code&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/code&gt;, no &lt;code&gt;scrollIntoView&lt;/code&gt; in an effect, no "is the user near the bottom" threshold to tune.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also renders with &lt;code&gt;role="log"&lt;/code&gt;, which tells screen readers this is a region where new content arrives in order, and announcements are polite, so they do not interrupt whatever the user is doing. The "Latest" pill is skipped by Tab while it is hidden.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake is to put the scroll container's height on a parent and let the container grow. Give &lt;code&gt;Conversation&lt;/code&gt; the flexible space (here &lt;code&gt;flex-1&lt;/code&gt; inside a full-height column) so it is the element that scrolls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Rendering turns: Message and StreamingText
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each turn is a &lt;code&gt;Message&lt;/code&gt;. User turns render as a compact bubble; assistant turns read as a full-width document with an avatar and quiet actions (copy, regenerate, rate) that appear on hover and stay visible on touch devices.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConversationContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isLast&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;assistant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MessageContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;assistant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StreamingText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;streaming&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isStreaming&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isLast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MessageContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConversationContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;StreamingText&lt;/code&gt; takes two props: the &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; so far and whether it is still &lt;code&gt;streaming&lt;/code&gt;. Each new chunk fades up out of a slight blur rather than popping in, a small caret marks the writing edge while streaming is true, and text already on screen never moves. That last property is the one that matters for long answers: people read streamed text as it arrives, and a paragraph that keeps shifting is tiring to follow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only the last assistant message should be marked as streaming. Earlier turns are history and should render as finished text with no caret.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. The composer: PromptInput
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;PromptInput&lt;/code&gt; is the composer, and it does more than a textarea:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;it grows with the text;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Enter sends and Shift+Enter adds a line, and IME composition is respected, so users typing in Japanese, Chinese or Korean do not send half-composed words;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;files can be attached by button, paste or drop (pass &lt;code&gt;attachments={false}&lt;/code&gt; to turn that off);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;while &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;"streaming"&lt;/code&gt;, Send becomes Stop, and Esc stops the run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That swap is the interruptibility problem solved. Pass the SDK's &lt;code&gt;stop&lt;/code&gt; function to &lt;code&gt;onStop&lt;/code&gt; and you are done. The component also accepts a &lt;code&gt;toolbar&lt;/code&gt; prop for extra controls, which is where a model picker belongs if your product lets people trade speed for quality. The &lt;code&gt;onSubmit&lt;/code&gt; callback receives &lt;code&gt;{ text, files }&lt;/code&gt;, so file uploads use the same handler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Handle the in-between states
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three small details separate a demo from a product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The gap before the first token.&lt;/strong&gt; Between pressing Send and the first chunk, the AI SDK reports &lt;code&gt;status === "submitted"&lt;/code&gt;. Treating that as streaming (as the code above does) means the Stop button appears immediately, which is exactly when users most often realise they asked the wrong question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Errors.&lt;/strong&gt; When &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;"error"&lt;/code&gt;, show it inside the conversation, next to the turn that failed, not as a toast that disappears. The user needs to see which message did not get an answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Empty state.&lt;/strong&gt; An empty &lt;code&gt;Conversation&lt;/code&gt; is a blank rectangle. A few suggested prompts tell people what the assistant is good at. Cuelark UI has a &lt;code&gt;Suggestions&lt;/code&gt; component for this; three suggestions work better than eight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Theming and motion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because everything installs as source styled with Tailwind CSS v4 tokens, the chat picks up your existing shadcn theme. If you want a starting point, the registry includes ready-made themes you can install with one command; browse them on the &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/themes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;theme gallery&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Motion is controlled globally by one CSS variable, &lt;code&gt;--cl-motion&lt;/code&gt;. Set it to &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; for a dense internal tool where you want nothing to move, or raise it on a marketing demo. A visitor's reduced-motion setting always wins over whatever you choose.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;:root&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;--cl-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


8. Accessibility checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you ship, test these with a keyboard and a screen reader:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You can reach the composer, type, send with Enter and stop with Esc without touching the mouse.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;New assistant text is announced politely, not token by token.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scrolling up during a reply stops auto-scroll, and the "Latest" pill is reachable by keyboard once visible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Focus is visible on every control, including message actions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;With reduced motion enabled at the OS level, nothing animates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The components cover these by default, but your layout can break them: a parent with &lt;code&gt;overflow: hidden&lt;/code&gt;, a custom focus style that removes outlines, or a wrapper that steals Enter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to build next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plain chat is the start. Once the model can think, call tools and ask permission, the interface needs more places to put things: reasoning that folds away, tool calls shown as facts, approvals that are hard to click through by accident. We cover those in the companion article on tool calls, reasoning and approvals, and you can see all of it working together in the &lt;a href="https://ui.cuelark.com/agents/research-agent" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;live research agent example&lt;/a&gt;, where every part of the interface can be inspected and copied.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is Cuelark UI free?&lt;/strong&gt; Yes. It is open source under the MIT licence, and the code is on GitHub at github.com/cuelark/ui.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do I have to use the Vercel AI SDK?&lt;/strong&gt; No. The components take plain props (&lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;streaming&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onSubmit&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onStop&lt;/code&gt;), so any streaming source works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can I change how the components look?&lt;/strong&gt; Yes. They are copied into your project as source, so you edit them like any other file in your codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Almost every Next.js app needs to send a few emails: a welcome email after signup, a magic link or password reset, a receipt. The App Router gives you several places to put that code, and they don't behave the same way when something is slow or fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide covers where the send should live, how to keep the API key off the client, how to avoid making users wait for your email provider, and the mistakes that lead to missing or duplicate emails. The examples use a generic &lt;code&gt;sendEmail()&lt;/code&gt; helper so you can plug in whichever provider you use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Keep the send on the server, always
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your email provider's API key can send mail as your domain. If it reaches the browser, anyone can use it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read it from &lt;code&gt;process.env.EMAIL_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; (no &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_&lt;/code&gt; prefix, which would inline it into client bundles).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put the helper in a module that only server code imports, and mark it so a mistaken client import fails the build:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// lib/email.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;server-only&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SendEmailInput&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SendEmailInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://api.your-email-provider.com/send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;EMAIL_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Email send failed: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;server-only&lt;/code&gt; package (&lt;code&gt;npm i server-only&lt;/code&gt;) makes the build fail if a Client Component imports this file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Pick the right entry point
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Trigger&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where to send from&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A form in your own UI (signup, invite a teammate, contact form)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;Server Action&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An external system calls you (Stripe webhook, auth provider hook, cron)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;route handler&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;app/api/.../route.ts&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Something that happens later (trial ending in 3 days, weekly digest)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;queue or scheduled job&lt;/strong&gt;, not a request handler&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Server Action: invite a teammate
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/team/actions.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;after&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;next/server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sendEmail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderInviteEmail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/emails/invite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;inviteTeammate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;FormData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Not signed in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLowerCase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Enter a valid email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;invite&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;invite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;teamId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;teamId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;invitedBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Respond to the user now; send the email after the response is done.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; invited you to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;teamName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderInviteEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;invite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;inviter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`invite-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;invite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two things to notice:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Authorization happens inside the action.&lt;/strong&gt; Server Actions are public HTTP endpoints. Anyone can call them with any form data, so check the session and validate input in the action itself, not only in the UI.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;after()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (from &lt;code&gt;next/server&lt;/code&gt;, stable since Next.js 15.1) runs the callback once the response has been sent. The user doesn't wait on your email provider's latency, and a slow provider doesn't hold the form in a pending state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Route handler: a webhook from your auth provider
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/api/webhooks/auth/route.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sendEmail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderWelcomeEmail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/emails/welcome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;verifyAuthWebhook&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/auth-webhooks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;verifyAuthWebhook&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// throws on a bad signature&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;user.created&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ignored&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Welcome to Acme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderWelcomeEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;firstName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`welcome-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Webhook senders retry when you return an error or time out, which is good for reliability and bad for duplicate emails. That's what the &lt;code&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/code&gt; is for (section 5).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Don't send from Client Components or &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's tempting to fire a "send welcome email" request from the client after signup succeeds. Don't:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The browser tab can close before the request goes out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React Strict Mode runs effects twice in development, so you'll see double emails locally and might "fix" the wrong thing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need a public endpoint for it, which anyone can call to make you send email.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Send from the server code that created the user, or from the webhook your auth provider calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Templates: React Email or plain HTML
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Email clients are not browsers. Outlook on Windows still renders with Word's engine, Gmail strips &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; blocks in some contexts, and flexbox and grid are unreliable. Two sane options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React Email&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;@react-email/components&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;@react-email/render&lt;/code&gt;): write templates as React components using email-safe primitives (&lt;code&gt;Section&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Row&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Column&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Button&lt;/code&gt;) and render them to an HTML string on the server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Provider-side templates&lt;/strong&gt;: design the email in your provider's editor and send a template ID plus variables. Non-developers can then edit copy without a deploy.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// emails/welcome.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Container&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Heading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@react-email/components&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@react-email/render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;WelcomeEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fontFamily&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Arial, sans-serif&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;backgroundColor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;#ffffff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Container&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;maxWidth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;560&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;24&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Heading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"h1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Welcome, &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Heading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Your workspace is ready. The fastest way to get value is to connect your data first.&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://app.acme.com/onboarding"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;backgroundColor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;#111&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;#fff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;12px 20px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Finish setup
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Container&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderWelcomeEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WelcomeEmail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// returns a Promise&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Whichever you choose, also send a plain-text version if your provider doesn't generate one, and keep the important action in a real link rather than only in an image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Make sends idempotent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Duplicates come from retries: a webhook delivered twice, a user double-submitting a form, a serverless function retried after a timeout. The fix is to give every logical email a stable key:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;welcome-${userId}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;invite-${inviteId}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;receipt-${invoiceId}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;password-reset-${tokenId}&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then either pass it to your provider as an idempotency key (many APIs accept one and return the original result for a repeated key), or record it in your database with a unique constraint and skip the send when the insert conflicts:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;inserted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sentEmail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createMany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`welcome-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;skipDuplicates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;inserted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// already sent&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


6. Know what &lt;code&gt;after()&lt;/code&gt; doesn't do
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;after()&lt;/code&gt; is great for "don't make the user wait". It is not a job queue:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the send throws, nothing retries it. Log the error, and alert on it for important emails.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On serverless platforms the work is tied to the lifetime of that invocation. Short sends are fine; long work belongs elsewhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It can't schedule anything for later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For emails that must arrive (password resets, receipts), either retry inside the callback with a short backoff, or enqueue a job (BullMQ, Inngest, QStash, Trigger.dev, your provider's own scheduling) and let the queue retry. For anything time-based (trial reminders, onboarding drips), use a scheduler or an email platform's automation instead of request handlers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Before you go live
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Send from your own domain with SPF, DKIM and DMARC set up, not the provider's shared test domain&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Separate sending subdomains for transactional and marketing email, if you send both&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] API key only in server env vars, &lt;code&gt;server-only&lt;/code&gt; on the email module&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Server Actions check the session and validate input&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Every email has an idempotency key&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Failures are logged and alerted, not swallowed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Test rendering in Gmail, Outlook and Apple Mail (or a rendering preview tool)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Using Sequenzy for this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how this looks with &lt;a href="https://www.sequenzy.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sequenzy&lt;/a&gt;, an email platform for SaaS. The &lt;code&gt;sendEmail&lt;/code&gt; helper above becomes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;server-only&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Sequenzy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;sequenzy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sequenzy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sequenzy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SEQUENZY_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendWelcome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;firstName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sequenzy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;transactional&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;welcome-email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// template designed in the dashboard&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variables&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;firstName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The same account also runs the onboarding and trial sequences from section 6, so time-based emails don't need their own queue. The &lt;a href="https://docs.sequenzy.com/quickstart" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;quick start&lt;/a&gt; takes about five minutes. Everything else in this guide applies whichever provider you choose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nakodo&lt;/a&gt; has 19 pages behind its login. It also has 19 &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; files. That is not a coincidence I noticed afterwards, it is a rule, and this is what checking it looks like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;d &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;find &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"src/app/(app)"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-name&lt;/span&gt; page.tsx | xargs &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-n1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-f&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/loading.tsx"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"no loading: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="err"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No output. Nineteen for 19. Together they are 885 lines, which is more code than any single page in the app, and the rest of this post is what is in them and the four decisions that shaped them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The primitive is called Bone, and it is hatched
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn's &lt;code&gt;Skeleton&lt;/code&gt; is 13 lines and ships with every install:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Skeleton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;data-slot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"skeleton"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;animate-pulse rounded-md bg-muted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It is imported exactly once in this repository, by the support panel, and that is the only place in the product where a grey pulsing rectangle appears.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything else uses this instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Loading skeletons. Hatching means "not yet observed", so each page's&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// skeleton is its own layout drawn in hatched shapes, with the text for&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// screen readers.&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Bone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CSSProperties&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;hatch h-3 rounded-sm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-hidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One hundred and fifty-nine uses. The difference between the two is one class. &lt;code&gt;hatch&lt;/code&gt; is a diagonal line texture from the app's stylesheet, and the reason it is the skeleton fill is written in the comment: the whole product is styled as a field guide, where a hatched shape means a specimen that has not been observed yet. A thing that has not loaded is not a grey box, it is an unobserved record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can see the texture itself without signing in. The &lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt; uses the same utility 36 times in its artwork, and if you grep its HTML you will find no &lt;code&gt;animate-pulse&lt;/code&gt; anywhere, because nothing on the public pages ever loads late:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://nakodo.app/ | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; hatch | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
36
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://nakodo.app/ | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; animate-pulse | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Three helpers on top of it, and 19 hand-drawn layouts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The shared file is 31 lines total. Besides &lt;code&gt;Bone&lt;/code&gt; there is &lt;code&gt;FieldBone&lt;/code&gt;, which is the label-over-input shape that appears 15 times across the settings and setup pages, and &lt;code&gt;Loading&lt;/code&gt;, the wrapper every one of the 19 files uses:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactNode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;motion-safe:animate-pulse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"sr-only"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three things are packed into those seven lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;role="status"&lt;/code&gt; with an &lt;code&gt;sr-only&lt;/code&gt; label, because a screen reader gets nothing at all from 40 hatched divs. The labels are written per page: "Loading your profile", "Loading the proposal", "Loading your campaigns". Twelve files in the repo carry a &lt;code&gt;role="status"&lt;/code&gt;, and this is the only one that covers 19 screens, because all 19 loading files render exactly one &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Loading&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;motion-safe:animate-pulse&lt;/code&gt; rather than &lt;code&gt;animate-pulse&lt;/code&gt;, so the one piece of motion in a skeleton is off for anybody who has asked their system to reduce motion. The individual &lt;code&gt;Bone&lt;/code&gt;s do not animate; the container does, once, for all of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And &lt;code&gt;aria-hidden="true"&lt;/code&gt; is on &lt;code&gt;Bone&lt;/code&gt; itself rather than on the wrapper, which means the author of a page skeleton cannot forget it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remaining 885 lines are the 19 layouts, and they are hand-drawn rather than generated. A settings page skeleton is the real card, with the real heading, and bones where the data goes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ProfileLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Loading your profile"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"space-y-5 rounded-lg border bg-card p-5 sm:p-6"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"space-y-1.5"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"font-sans text-base font-semibold tracking-normal"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Profile&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Bone&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"w-80 max-w-full"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;h2&amp;gt;Profile&amp;lt;/h2&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; is the point of the whole approach. Eleven of the 19 loading files render at least one real heading, because the heading is not loading. It is a constant in the source of the page above it. Boning out text the server already knows makes the page flicker from a grey smear into a word for no reason, and it makes the skeleton say less than it could.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost is that the 19 files are 19 duplications of a layout, and nothing enforces that they stay in step with the pages they shadow. Three of them have grown large enough to extract into a shared component next to the page it belongs to, which is where &lt;code&gt;skeleton.tsx&lt;/code&gt; files start appearing: the setup wizard's step strip, the replies list and the proposal form each have one, used by both the loading file and the page itself for its own internal pending states.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The function I would keep in any project
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Rows fade out towards the bottom, so a skeleton never reads as a full list.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fade&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Seven call sites, all of them lists: campaigns, creators, conversations, support threads, messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A skeleton list has to pick a number of rows, and whatever it picks is a lie, because the real answer is "three" or "forty" and nobody knows yet. Three solid rows read as "you have three campaigns". The same three rows at 100, 77 and 53 per cent opacity read as "a list, going on past here", which is both honest and the thing the eye expects of a list that is still arriving. The constant 0.7 means the last row keeps 30 per cent of its opacity, so it fades rather than disappearing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is one line, it took two minutes, and it is the single change that made the loading states stop looking like fake content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The six Suspense boundaries, four of which have no fallback
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; is the route-level boundary that Next gives you for free. There are six explicit &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Suspense&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; boundaries on top of it, and they split cleanly in two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two have real fallbacks, both in the campaign layout:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;HeaderSkeleton&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That layout wraps every campaign page, so without a boundary the layout's own queries would delay the page's &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; from appearing at all. With it, the frame arrives immediately and the header and the tab strip fill in. The tab strip's fallback is interesting: it is not a skeleton at all, it is the real &lt;code&gt;CampaignTabs&lt;/code&gt; component with &lt;code&gt;kind={null}&lt;/code&gt;, because the tabs are known from the URL and only their labels depend on data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other four have no fallback at all:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DeletedNotice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Suspense&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; with no fallback renders nothing until the child is ready, and all four children are client components that read &lt;code&gt;useSearchParams&lt;/code&gt;. In the App Router, reading the search params in a client component opts the page out of static rendering unless it sits inside a boundary. One of these four is on the public &lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt;, which is prerendered and served from a CDN: the notice that confirms an account was deleted reads &lt;code&gt;?deleted=1&lt;/code&gt;, and without that empty boundary a one-line confirmation message would make the whole marketing home page dynamic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the same API is doing two unrelated jobs in one codebase. With a fallback it is "show this while you wait". Without one it is "this subtree reads the URL, do not let it infect the page". Both are correct, both are three lines, and only one of them is about loading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the public half does instead
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of the above exists on the 76 pages in &lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/sitemap.xml" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the sitemap&lt;/a&gt;. They have no &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, no skeleton and no spinner, because there is nothing to wait for: they are built at deploy time from content that lives in the repository, and the server sends finished HTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That asymmetry is worth stating plainly, because it is the actual reason the 885 lines are affordable. Loading states are a cost you pay per page that reads a database, and the 76 public URLs do not read one. Go and click around &lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/guides" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the guides&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://nakodo.app/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt; and nothing ever fades in, because nothing was ever absent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is one more exception, and it is not static. The operator's panel at &lt;code&gt;/admin&lt;/code&gt; is seven pages, every one of them reading the database, and it has zero &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; files. Six hundred milliseconds of nothing on a route only I can reach never once annoyed me enough to write a skeleton for it, which is the most honest sentence in this post: 885 lines of loading state is not politeness, it is what you build for the pages other people wait on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The numbers, in one place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Thing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Count&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pages behind the login&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lines of loading UI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;885&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Bone&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; uses&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;159&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;FieldBone&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; uses&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;fade()&lt;/code&gt; call sites&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Files with &lt;code&gt;role="status"&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; files in &lt;code&gt;/admin&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;shadcn &lt;code&gt;Skeleton&lt;/code&gt; imports&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicit &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Of those, with no fallback&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The thing I did not expect when I counted was the last two rows. I think of &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; as the loading API and &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt; as sugar over it, and in this codebase it is the other way round: the route convention does all of the loading, and two thirds of the explicit boundaries are there to keep a page static.&lt;/p&gt;
